window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["lambda"]=[{"id":"lambda-f0001","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"What is AWS Lambda?","a":"<p>A serverless, event-driven compute service that runs code without you provisioning or managing servers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0002","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"What does \"serverless\" mean for Lambda?","a":"<p>Three things:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Pay-as-you-go.</li>\n  <li>No infrastructure management: AWS handles servers, scaling, patching and availability.</li>\n  <li>Automatic scaling up and down with traffic.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0003","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"What are the three steps of how Lambda works?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><strong>Upload code</strong> in a supported language (JavaScript, Python, Java, C#, Go and more).</li>\n  <li><strong>Configure triggers</strong>: state changes, HTTP requests or AWS service events (for example an S3 bucket event).</li>\n  <li><strong>Execution</strong>: Lambda runs the code when triggered. The code can run business logic, call other AWS services or call external APIs.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0004","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"In Node.js, what does a minimal Lambda handler that logs the event and returns status 200 look like?","a":"<p>It is an exported async <code>handler</code> that logs the event and returns a response object:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    console.log(&#x27;Event:&#x27;, JSON.stringify(event));\n    const response = {\n        statusCode: 200,\n        body: JSON.stringify(&#x27;Processed event successfully!&#x27;)\n    };\n    return response;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0005","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"What are the main use cases for AWS Lambda?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Data processing</strong>: enrich data streams or run real-time analytics (for example Kinesis records).</li>\n  <li><strong>Web and mobile backends</strong>: replace EC2 for HTTP requests (API Gateway + Lambda for REST APIs).</li>\n  <li><strong>Infrastructure management</strong>: trigger code that manages cloud resources, such as stopping unused EC2 instances.</li>\n  <li><strong>Event-driven architectures</strong>: run business logic in response to events.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0006","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"How can a Lambda function stop an EC2 instance in Node.js?","a":"<p>Create an EC2 client with the AWS SDK and call <code>stopInstances</code> with the instance IDs:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const AWS = require(&#x27;aws-sdk&#x27;);\nconst ec2 = new AWS.EC2();\nexports.handler = async () =&gt; {\n    const params = { InstanceIds: [&#x27;i-1234567890abcdef0&#x27;] };\n    await ec2.stopInstances(params).promise();\n    return &#x27;Instances stopped&#x27;;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0007","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"Why is Lambda a poor fit for extreme real-time needs?","a":"<p>Lambda typically answers in double-digit milliseconds, not single-digit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0008","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"Why is Lambda a poor fit for specialized hardware tasks such as video rendering?","a":"<p>Lambda runs on generic hardware, which does not suit those tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0009","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"Why is Lambda not recommended for 100% reliability systems such as life support?","a":"<p>Because of third-party dependencies, it cannot be relied on for critical systems that need 100% reliability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0010","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"How is AWS Lambda priced?","a":"<p>You pay for the number of requests and for GB-seconds. GB-seconds are allocated memory (GB) multiplied by execution duration, billed per 1 ms. A monthly free tier exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0011","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"Why can allocating more memory still lower your Lambda cost?","a":"<p>More memory costs more per ms, but the function can finish faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0012","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"Which Lambda charges are billed separately from requests and GB-seconds?","a":"<p>Provisioned Concurrency and ephemeral storage larger than the default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0013","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"What inputs does the pricing example use for 1,000,000 requests a month?","a":"<p>1,000,000 requests/month, 200 ms duration per request and 128 MB memory:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Requests: 1,000,000/month\nDuration: 200ms/request\nMemory: 128MB</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0014","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"What is AWS Lambda, and how does serverless compute differ from EC2?","a":"<p>Lambda is a serverless, event-driven compute service that runs your code in response to events without you provisioning or managing servers. You upload code, define a trigger, and AWS handles the servers, scaling, patching and availability. With EC2 you rent and manage a server (OS, scaling, patching) and pay while it runs. With Lambda you pay only per request and per ms of execution, it scales to zero when idle, and it scales out automatically per request.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0015","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"Explain the concept of event-driven execution in Lambda.","a":"<p>Lambda runs only when an event occurs: an HTTP call via API Gateway, an object uploaded to S3, a message in SQS, a DynamoDB stream record or a scheduled EventBridge rule. Each event reaches the handler as the <code>event</code> object. No event means no execution and no cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0016","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"What is the execution model of AWS Lambda (stateless compute)?","a":"<p>Each invocation is stateless. Do not rely on in-memory data persisting across requests, because AWS may create a fresh execution environment at any time. Keep state in external stores (DynamoDB, S3, RDS, ElastiCache). The environment can be reused for warm invocations, so anything outside the handler (SDK clients, connections) can be cached opportunistically.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># WRONG: relying on in-memory state across requests\ncounter = 0\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    global counter\n    counter += 1          # NOT reliable: new env resets this to 0\n    return counter\n\n# RIGHT: persist state externally (e.g., DynamoDB atomic counter)\nimport boto3\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    r = ddb.update_item(\n        TableName=&quot;Counters&quot;, Key={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: &quot;hits&quot;}},\n        UpdateExpression=&quot;ADD #c :one&quot;,\n        ExpressionAttributeNames={&quot;#c&quot;: &quot;count&quot;},\n        ExpressionAttributeValues={&quot;:one&quot;: {&quot;N&quot;: &quot;1&quot;}},\n        ReturnValues=&quot;UPDATED_NEW&quot;)\n    return r[&quot;Attributes&quot;][&quot;count&quot;][&quot;N&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0017","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What are the core components of an AWS Lambda function configuration?","a":"<p>Handler (entry point), runtime (execution environment), execution role (IAM permissions), memory (128 MB to 10,240 MB, which also sets CPU), timeout (up to 15 min), the deployment package (zip or container image), environment variables, trigger or event source config, concurrency settings, layers, ephemeral <code>/tmp</code> storage size and optional VPC config.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># SAM: the core configuration knobs in one place\nMyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Handler: app.lambda_handler      # entry point (file.function)\n    Runtime: python3.12              # execution environment\n    MemorySize: 512                  # MB, also scales CPU/network\n    Timeout: 30                      # seconds (max 900)\n    Role: !GetAtt MyRole.Arn         # IAM execution role\n    EphemeralStorage: { Size: 512 }  # /tmp size in MB (512-10240)\n    Environment:\n      Variables: { TABLE_NAME: Users }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># The handler receives (event, context)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 200, &quot;body&quot;: &quot;ok&quot;}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0018","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"Which native runtimes does Lambda support out of the box?","a":"<p>Node.js, Python, Java, .NET (C#), Ruby and Go (via the provided runtime family). Anything else runs through Custom Runtimes using the Runtime API, or by packaging an OCI container image.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0019","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the max execution timeout for a single invocation?","a":"<p>900 seconds (15 minutes).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0020","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the maximum payload size for synchronous vs asynchronous calls?","a":"<p>Synchronous: 6 MB request/response. Asynchronous: 256 KB event payload.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0021","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"How does the pricing model work?","a":"<p>You pay for (a) the number of requests and (b) GB-seconds = allocated memory (GB) x execution duration (billed per 1 ms). More memory costs more per ms but can finish faster. There is a monthly free tier. Provisioned Concurrency and ephemeral storage above the default are billed separately.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0022","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the default <code>/tmp</code> storage and its maximum?","a":"<p>Default 512 MB, configurable up to 10,240 MB (10 GB). It is scratch space that may persist across warm invocations but is not durable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0023","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"Zip archives vs OCI container images?","a":"<p>Zip: upload code and dependencies as an archive (50 MB zipped for direct upload, 250 MB unzipped). Container image: an OCI image up to 10 GB, giving room for large dependencies or ML models and letting you use familiar container tooling. Zip is simpler and faster for small functions. Containers suit large or complex dependency sets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0024","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"What are the unzipped code and dependency size limits?","a":"<p>Zip deployment: 250 MB unzipped (code and layers combined). Direct zip upload: 50 MB, larger packages go via S3. Container image: up to 10 GB.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0025","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"What are Custom Runtimes, and how do you implement one with the Runtime API?","a":"<p>A custom runtime lets you run a language Lambda does not support natively. You provide a <code>bootstrap</code> executable that implements the Runtime API loop: poll <code>/runtime/invocation/next</code> for events, run your handler, and POST the result to <code>/runtime/invocation/{id}/response</code> (or <code>/error</code>). Ship it as a zip (on the <code>provided.al2023</code> or <code>provided.al2</code> base) or as a container image.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>#!/bin/sh\n# bootstrap: implements the Runtime API loop (provided.al2023)\nset -euo pipefail\nwhile true; do\n  # 1. Get the next invocation\n  EVENT=$(curl -sS &quot;http://$AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API/2018-06-01/runtime/invocation/next&quot; -D /tmp/h)\n  REQ_ID=$(grep -Fi Lambda-Runtime-Aws-Request-Id /tmp/h | tr -d &#x27;[:space:]&#x27; | cut -d: -f2)\n\n  # 2. Run your handler logic\n  RESPONSE=&quot;Hello from custom runtime: $EVENT&quot;\n\n  # 3. POST the result back\n  curl -sS &quot;http://$AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API/2018-06-01/runtime/invocation/$REQ_ID/response&quot; \\\n    -d &quot;$RESPONSE&quot;\ndone</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0026","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"How does Lambda scale under the hood when traffic spikes?","a":"<p>Lambda creates a new execution environment per concurrent request. When requests arrive faster than existing environments free up, it adds more, up to your concurrency limit. Scaling starts with an initial burst capacity, then continues at a controlled rate (for example 500 additional environments per function per minute in supported regions) until it reaches the account or function limit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0027","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"What is Firecracker, and how does AWS use microVMs for Lambda?","a":"<p>Firecracker is AWS's open-source virtualization technology that launches lightweight microVMs in milliseconds. Lambda uses microVMs to give each execution environment strong VM-level isolation between functions and accounts while keeping startup fast and memory overhead tiny. It combines container-like speed with VM-like security.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0028","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"What is the difference between an event source mapping and a direct trigger?","a":"<p>A direct trigger is a service invoking Lambda by calling the Lambda API (push model), for example API Gateway, S3 or SNS. An event source mapping (ESM) is a Lambda-managed poller that pulls records from a source (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, Kafka), batches them and invokes your function. With an ESM, Lambda owns the polling loop. With a direct trigger, the source pushes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0029","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"Which user should you sign in with to the Lambda console, and why does the region matter?","a":"<p>Sign in with an IAM admin user, not root. Lambda is region-specific: functions are isolated by region and account, so check the region first when a function seems missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0030","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"What is the default account concurrency limit shown for Lambda in the console?","a":"<p>1,000 concurrent executions, and it can be raised. Account-level metrics sit on the dashboard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0031","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"What do the Layers, Triggers and ARN parts of a Lambda function page show?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Layers</strong>: manage dependencies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Triggers</strong>: events that invoke the function (for example API Gateway).</li>\n  <li><strong>ARN</strong>: the unique identifier of the function.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0032","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"What is an example of a Lambda function ARN?","a":"<p><code>arn:aws:lambda:eu-west-1:123456789012:function:helloWorld</code></p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0033","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"What do the Code, Configuration, Monitoring and Test tabs of a function do?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Code</strong>: source code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Configuration</strong>: memory (affects performance and cost), triggers, permissions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: metrics, logs and traces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Test</strong>: run the function directly with a test event.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0034","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"What does the default Lambda execution role allow?","a":"<p>Only CloudWatch Logs access. Functions start with minimal permissions, so add only what is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0035","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"Which IAM actions does the default Lambda logging policy allow?","a":"<p><code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Action&quot;: [\n  &quot;logs:CreateLogGroup&quot;,\n  &quot;logs:CreateLogStream&quot;,\n  &quot;logs:PutLogEvents&quot;\n],</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0036","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"What are the steps to create and test a first function in the Lambda console?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Choose <strong>Create function</strong> &gt; <strong>Author from scratch</strong> (other options: blueprint, container image).</li>\n  <li>Set a name (<code>my-lambda-function</code>), runtime (Node.js 18.x) and architecture (Intel x86_64 or Graviton).</li>\n  <li>On the <strong>Code</strong> tab edit the handler and click <strong>Deploy</strong> to make the change live.</li>\n  <li>On the <strong>Test</strong> tab create a test event and run it. No trigger is needed to test.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0037","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"Which runtimes and architectures can you choose when creating a function?","a":"<p>Node.js 18.x, but Python, Java and custom runtimes also work. Architecture is Intel x86_64 or Graviton.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0038","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"What must you click on the Code tab to make a handler change live?","a":"<p><strong>Deploy</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0039","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"What does a Lambda test result show?","a":"<p>The response, the logged event and a <code>REPORT</code> line with duration, billed duration and memory used. Use that data later to tune the function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0040","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"What does the <code>REPORT</code> line of a Lambda test result look like?","a":"<p>It lists duration, billed duration, memory size and max memory used:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>REPORT Duration: 123.45 ms Billed Duration: 124 ms Memory Size: 128 MB Max Memory Used: 50 MB</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0041","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"What is a simple test event JSON for a first Lambda function?","a":"<p>Three key/value pairs:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;key1&quot;: &quot;value1&quot;,\n  &quot;key2&quot;: &quot;value2&quot;,\n  &quot;key3&quot;: &quot;value3&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0042","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What are the three parts of the Lambda programming model?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><strong>Triggers</strong> invoke the function: API Gateway (HTTP requests), DynamoDB (record changes), S3 (file creation or modification), SQS (new messages).</li>\n  <li><strong>Handler</strong> is the entry point.</li>\n  <li><strong>Function code</strong> is your business logic: API calls, data processing, calls to other AWS services.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0043","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What are the inputs of a Lambda handler?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>event</strong>: data from the trigger, shape varies by trigger.</li>\n  <li><strong>context</strong>: runtime information such as function name and remaining time.</li>\n  <li><strong>callback</strong> (optional): returns data to the invoker. Not needed with async/await.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0044","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"How do you return a response with a callback in a Node.js handler?","a":"<p>Call <code>callback(null, response)</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event, context, callback) =&gt; {\n    callback(null, response); // Use callback for synchronous responses\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0045","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What two things does the Lambda <code>context</code> object offer?","a":"<p><code>context.functionName</code> and <code>context.getRemainingTimeInMillis()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0046","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"How do you see the <code>event</code> a trigger sent to your Lambda function?","a":"<p>Log it with <code>console.log</code>, then read it in CloudWatch. Parse the <code>event</code> carefully per trigger.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0047","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What does an API Gateway GET event look like in the simplified form?","a":"<p>It has <code>httpMethod</code>, <code>path</code> and <code>headers</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;httpMethod&quot;: &quot;GET&quot;,\n  &quot;path&quot;: &quot;/example&quot;,</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0048","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What does an SQS event look like in the simplified form?","a":"<p>A <code>Records</code> array whose items have <code>body</code> and <code>messageId</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Records&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;body&quot;: &quot;Message content&quot;,\n      &quot;messageId&quot;: &quot;1234567890&quot;\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0049","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"What are the three Lambda execution models?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><strong>Synchronous</strong>: the invoker waits for the response (blocking). Example: API Gateway handling HTTP requests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous</strong>: the invoker gets an acknowledgment and does not wait. Example: S3 triggering a function on file upload.</li>\n  <li><strong>Poll stream-based</strong>: Lambda polls a stream or queue (SQS, Kinesis) and invokes the function when records are available.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0050","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"How does a synchronous API Gateway handler return its result?","a":"<p>It returns an object with <code>statusCode</code> and <code>body</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    return {\n        statusCode: 200,\n        body: JSON.stringify(&#x27;Processed HTTP request&#x27;)\n    };\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0051","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"How does an S3-triggered (asynchronous) handler read the S3 information?","a":"<p>From <code>event.Records[0].s3</code>. No return value is needed:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    console.log(&#x27;S3 Event:&#x27;, event.Records[0].s3);\n    // Process file, no return needed\n    return &#x27;File processed&#x27;;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0052","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"How does an SQS (poll-based) handler process messages?","a":"<p>It loops over <code>event.Records</code> and reads each <code>record.body</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    for (const record of event.Records) {\n        console.log(&#x27;SQS Message:&#x27;, record.body);\n        // Process message\n    }\n    return &#x27;Messages processed&#x27;;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0053","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"What do the common Lambda triggers do?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>API Gateway</strong>: routes an HTTP request (for example <code>GET /hello</code>) to the function. Used for serverless web backends.</li>\n  <li><strong>S3</strong>: fires on object create, update or delete. Used to enrich files or start workflow steps (process images on upload).</li>\n  <li><strong>DynamoDB</strong> (Streams): fires when a record is created, updated or deleted. Used for transactions or stored-procedure-like logic in a NoSQL database.</li>\n  <li><strong>SQS</strong>: fires on new queue messages. Decouples functions for scalable architectures.</li>\n  <li><strong>Kinesis</strong>: fires on new stream events. Handles high-volume real-time data (IoT, analytics).</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0054","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"Why must a Lambda function validate the <code>event</code> it receives?","a":"<p>Each trigger gives a different <code>event</code> shape.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0055","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"What IAM permissions does a function need for its triggering service?","a":"<p>Permission to the triggering service (for example <code>s3:GetObject</code>, <code>sqs:ReceiveMessage</code>), scoped finely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0056","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"How does a Lambda function read the uploaded file name from an S3 event?","a":"<p>From <code>event.Records[0].s3.object.key</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    const file = event.Records[0].s3.object.key;\n    console.log(&#x27;New file:&#x27;, file);\n    return `Processed ${file}`;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0057","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"How does a Lambda function read records from a DynamoDB stream event?","a":"<p>It loops over <code>event.Records</code> and reads <code>record.dynamodb</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    for (const record of event.Records) {\n        console.log(&#x27;DynamoDB Record:&#x27;, record.dynamodb);\n    }\n    return &#x27;Processed records&#x27;;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0058","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"How does a Lambda function decode Kinesis data?","a":"<p>Kinesis data is base64 encoded. Decode it with <code>Buffer.from(record.kinesis.data, 'base64').toString()</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const data = Buffer.from(record.kinesis.data, &#x27;base64&#x27;).toString();</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0059","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"What IAM statement lets a function read objects from an S3 bucket?","a":"<p>Allow the <code>s3:GetObject</code> action on the bucket's objects:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;],\n&quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0060","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What is Amazon API Gateway?","a":"<p>A fully managed service for creating, publishing, maintaining, monitoring and securing APIs at scale. It is the front door to backends (Lambda, EC2, DynamoDB, Kinesis or public endpoints) and handles up to 200,000 concurrent API calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0061","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What does API Gateway manage, and how is it priced?","a":"<p>It manages traffic, authorization, access control, monitoring and API versions. Pricing is pay-per-use (API calls and data out), with no minimums.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0062","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What protocols do clients use to send requests to API Gateway?","a":"<p>HTTP or WebSocket requests to its endpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0063","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What can you do with API Gateway and Lambda?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Route HTTP requests to a function by path and method.</li>\n  <li>WebSockets for serverless real-time chat (with Lambda and DynamoDB for sessions).</li>\n  <li>Direct DynamoDB CRUD through Velocity Template Language (VTL) mapping, with no Lambda in between.</li>\n  <li>Authentication through Amazon Cognito or Lambda authorizers.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0064","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What does an API Gateway VTL mapping template for a DynamoDB key look like?","a":"<p>It builds the DynamoDB request from <code>$input.params</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Vtl</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;TableName&quot;: &quot;MyTable&quot;,\n  &quot;Key&quot;: {\n    &quot;id&quot;: { &quot;S&quot;: &quot;$input.params(&#x27;id&#x27;)&quot; }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0065","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What must a Lambda authorizer return?","a":"<p>A <code>principalId</code> and a <code>policyDocument</code> that allows (or denies) <code>execute-api:Invoke</code> on <code>event.methodArn</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return {\n    principalId: &#x27;user&#x27;,\n    policyDocument: {\n        Version: &#x27;2012-10-17&#x27;,\n        Statement: [\n            {\n                Effect: &#x27;Allow&#x27;,\n                Action: &#x27;execute-api:Invoke&#x27;,\n                Resource: event.methodArn\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0066","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"Where does a Lambda authorizer read the token from?","a":"<p>From <code>event.headers.Authorization</code>. It validates the token with Cognito or custom logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0067","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"How do you add an API Gateway trigger to a Lambda function?","a":"<p>In the function choose <strong>Add trigger</strong> &gt; <strong>API Gateway</strong>, create a new API with security <strong>Open</strong> (no authentication, for learning only) and click <strong>Add</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0068","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"What does the API Gateway trigger show after you add it?","a":"<p>An endpoint such as <code>https://&lt;api-id&gt;.execute-api.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/default</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0069","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"What are the settings of the new API created by the trigger?","a":"<p>Type HTTP, authorization none, CORS off, any HTTP method, with a stage (<code>default</code>) for versioned deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0070","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"Can one API Gateway API trigger several functions?","a":"<p>Yes, through different resource paths.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0071","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"What do production APIs need that the learning API does not have?","a":"<p>Authentication (Cognito or Lambda authorizers) and CORS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0072","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"Where do query string parameters arrive in a Lambda event from API Gateway, and what must you check?","a":"<p>In <code>event.queryStringParameters</code>. Always check they exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0073","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"How does a handler return <code>Hello &lt;name&gt;!</code> from the <code>name</code> query parameter?","a":"<p>It reads <code>event.queryStringParameters || {}</code> and builds the message:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    const queryParams = event.queryStringParameters || {};\n    const name = queryParams.name || null;\n    let message = &#x27;Hello from Lambda!&#x27;;\n    if (name) {\n        message = `Hello ${name}!`;\n    }\n    return {\n        statusCode: 200,\n        body: JSON.stringify(message)\n    };\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0074","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"What does the query-string greeting function return for <code>?name=Marcia</code> and for no parameter?","a":"<p><code>?name=Marcia</code> returns <code>Hello Marcia!</code>. No parameter returns <code>Hello from Lambda!</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0075","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"Why use Postman to test a Lambda API instead of a browser?","a":"<p>Browsers only send simple <code>GET</code> requests. Postman supports all HTTP methods plus headers, query parameters, bodies and authorization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0076","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"Why must the function parse <code>event.body</code> itself when the trigger uses <code>ANY</code>?","a":"<p>A trigger configured with <code>ANY</code> accepts any method, and API Gateway delivers the body as a string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0077","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"How does a handler read <code>name</code> from a POST JSON body as well as the query string?","a":"<p>If there is no query <code>name</code> and <code>event.body</code> exists, it parses the body with <code>JSON.parse</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if (!name &amp;&amp; event.body) {\n    const body = JSON.parse(event.body);\n    name = body.name || null;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0078","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"What does the API Gateway event look like for a POST with a JSON name?","a":"<p><code>httpMethod</code> is <code>POST</code>, and <code>body</code> is a JSON string:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;httpMethod&quot;: &quot;POST&quot;,\n  &quot;queryStringParameters&quot;: {},\n  &quot;body&quot;: &quot;{\\&quot;name\\&quot;: \\&quot;Marcia\\&quot;}&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0079","card":"q-lambda-016-challenge-add-two-numbers-from-a-post-re","sec":"lambda-s017","q":"What does the add-two-numbers challenge function (<code>Challenge1</code>) do?","a":"<p>It has an open API Gateway trigger. It reads <code>value1</code> and <code>value2</code> from the query string, validates them and returns the sum or an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0080","card":"q-lambda-016-challenge-add-two-numbers-from-a-post-re","sec":"lambda-s017","q":"How does the challenge function turn the query values into numbers and check them?","a":"<p>It uses <code>parseInt</code> and <code>isNaN</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const num1 = parseInt(value1);\nconst num2 = parseInt(value2);\nif (!isNaN(num1) &amp;&amp; !isNaN(num2)) {\n    message = `Result: ${num1 + num2}`;\n} else {\n    message = &#x27;Invalid numbers provided&#x27;;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0081","card":"q-lambda-016-challenge-add-two-numbers-from-a-post-re","sec":"lambda-s017","q":"What are the four test results for the add-two-numbers challenge?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>No parameters returns <code>Value1 and Value2 are needed</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>?value1=10&amp;value2=14</code> returns <code>Result: 24</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>?value1=10</code> returns the needed message.</li>\n  <li><code>?value1=abc&amp;value2=14</code> returns <code>Invalid numbers provided</code>.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0082","card":"q-lambda-016-challenge-add-two-numbers-from-a-post-re","sec":"lambda-s017","q":"What is the default message in the add-two-numbers challenge before validation?","a":"<p><code>Value1 and Value2 are needed</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0083","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"Walk through the lifecycle of an execution environment (Init, Invoke, Shutdown).","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Init</strong>: AWS creates the microVM, downloads code, starts the runtime and runs your initialization code (everything outside the handler). Extensions register here.</li>\n  <li><strong>Invoke</strong>: the handler runs for each event. A warm environment skips Init and goes straight here.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shutdown</strong>: when the environment is idle or reclaimed, Lambda sends a shutdown signal and extensions get a short window to clean up. Your handler is not called.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Python: code placement maps to the lifecycle\nimport boto3\n\n# --- INIT phase: runs ONCE per environment, reused across warm invokes ---\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)   # create clients here, not in the handler\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    # --- INVOKE phase: runs on EVERY event ---\n    return ddb.get_item(TableName=&quot;Users&quot;, Key={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: event[&quot;id&quot;]}})</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0084","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"What happens during Shutdown, and how can you use extension hooks?","a":"<p>When Lambda reclaims an idle environment, external extensions receive a <code>SHUTDOWN</code> event with a limited window (typically up to about 2 s) to flush logs and metrics, close connections or export telemetry. Use these hooks to clean up gracefully instead of losing buffered data.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0085","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What causes a cold start, and how does it affect latency?","a":"<p>A cold start happens when no warm environment is available, so Lambda must run the Init phase (spin up the microVM, load the runtime, run your init code) before invoking. This adds latency, from tens of ms to a few seconds depending on runtime and package size. Warm invocations skip it. Cold starts are most visible with spiky traffic, large packages, VPC-attached functions and heavy runtimes such as Java and .NET.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0086","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What is Provisioned Concurrency, and how does it remove cold starts?","a":"<p>Provisioned Concurrency (PC) pre-initializes a set number of execution environments and keeps them warm, so invocations skip Init and get consistent low latency. You pay for the provisioned capacity whether it is used or not. It is often paired with Application Auto Scaling and used on a function alias or version.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-provisioned-concurrency-config \\\n  --function-name my-func --qualifier PROD \\\n  --provisioned-concurrent-executions 20</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0087","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What is Lambda SnapStart, and how does it work for Java?","a":"<p>SnapStart (first for Java, now broader) snapshots the memory and disk of the initialized environment after Init, encrypts and caches it, and restores from that snapshot on invoke instead of re-running Init. It can cut Java cold starts sharply at no extra cost. Caveat: uniqueness. Anything created once at Init (random seeds, connection tokens) is captured in the snapshot, so use runtime hooks (<code>afterRestore</code>) to regenerate per-restore state.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Removes cold start?</th><th>Extra cost?</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Provisioned Concurrency</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (pre-warmed capacity)</td><td>Predictable, latency-sensitive traffic</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SnapStart</td><td>Largely</td><td>No</td><td>Java (and supported runtimes)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Smaller package / lazy load</td><td>Reduces</td><td>No</td><td>Every function</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0088","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"How does package size affect cold start duration?","a":"<p>Larger packages take longer to download and unpack during Init. Keep them lean: prune unused dependencies, use layers for shared libraries, tree-shake or minify, and avoid bundling large assets you do not need at runtime.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0089","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"How do compiled languages (Go, Rust) compare with interpreted ones (Python, Node.js) for cold starts?","a":"<p>Go and Rust usually have very fast cold starts and low memory overhead because they ship a native binary with no heavy runtime to boot. Python and Node.js are also fairly light. Java and .NET historically have the slowest cold starts because of JVM/CLR startup and class loading (mitigated by SnapStart and Provisioned Concurrency).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0090","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"How does global variable initialization work between warm invocations?","a":"<p>Variables initialized outside the handler live in the execution environment and persist across warm invocations. That is how you cache SDK clients, DB connections and config. Do not rely on it for correctness (environments are transient), but use it for performance.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Node.js: cached across warm invokes\nconst cache = {};                    // survives between invocations\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n  if (!cache.config) cache.config = await loadConfig(); // loaded once\n  return cache.config[event.key];\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0091","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"Why instantiate DB connections or SDK clients outside the handler?","a":"<p>Init runs once per environment and the objects are then reused across warm invocations. Creating a connection or client inside the handler rebuilds it on every request, adding latency and risking connection exhaustion.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0092","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"How do you reuse HTTP connection pools across invocations?","a":"<p>Create the HTTP client or agent outside the handler so keep-alive pools survive warm invocations. For the AWS SDK, keep-alive is the default in SDK v3 and is enabled by <code>AWS_NODEJS_CONNECTION_REUSE_ENABLED=1</code> in v2.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Node.js: reuse a keep-alive HTTP pool across invocations\nconst https = require(&quot;https&quot;);\nconst agent = new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 50 });\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n  return callApi(event, { agent });  // pool persists while env is warm\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0093","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"How does memory affect CPU, network bandwidth and cost?","a":"<p>Memory is the one dial that also scales CPU and network proportionally. More memory means more vCPU and bandwidth and often faster execution. Cost per ms rises with memory, but faster completion can lower total GB-seconds, so tuning memory can reduce both latency and cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0094","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"What tool tunes a function's memory (for example AWS Lambda Power Tuning)?","a":"<p>AWS Lambda Power Tuning is a Step Functions state machine that runs the function at several memory settings and charts cost against speed so you pick the sweet spot. You can also compare CloudWatch <code>REPORT</code> logs (Max Memory Used, Duration) across settings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0095","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"How does lazy vs eager loading of libraries affect execution time?","a":"<p>Eager loading (importing everything at Init) lengthens cold starts but makes every invoke fast. Lazy loading (importing a heavy module only when a code path needs it) shortens cold starts and avoids paying for libraries you rarely use, at the cost of a one-time hit on the first invoke that needs them. Choose by how often each path runs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Lazy load a heavy dependency only when needed\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if event.get(&quot;needs_pandas&quot;):\n        import pandas as pd          # imported only on this path\n        return process(pd, event)\n    return quick_path(event)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0096","card":"q-lambda-022-latency-fixes-at-a-glance","sec":"lambda-s023","q":"What are the four quick fixes for Lambda latency?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Enable X-Ray tracing and find the slow call.</li>\n  <li>Raise memory (more CPU).</li>\n  <li>Optimize or lazy-load initialization code.</li>\n  <li>Use Provisioned Concurrency or SnapStart for cold starts.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0097","card":"q-lambda-022-latency-fixes-at-a-glance","sec":"lambda-s023","q":"What is the first step to fix a slow Lambda function?","a":"<p>Enable X-Ray tracing and find the slow call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0098","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"What are the three invocation models?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Synchronous</strong>: the caller invokes and waits for the response (<code>RequestResponse</code>). Errors return to the caller.</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous</strong>: the caller hands the event to Lambda's internal queue and gets an immediate acknowledgment (<code>Event</code>). Lambda invokes and handles retries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Event Source Mapping (ESM)</strong>: Lambda polls a source (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, Kafka), batches records and invokes the function.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda invoke --function-name f --invocation-type RequestResponse out.json  # sync\naws lambda invoke --function-name f --invocation-type Event out.json            # async</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0099","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"Three services that invoke Lambda synchronously?","a":"<p>API Gateway, Application Load Balancer (ALB) and a direct <code>Invoke</code> call from the SDK or CLI. Also Cognito, Lex and Step Functions optimized <code>Task</code> states.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0100","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"Three services that invoke Lambda asynchronously?","a":"<p>S3 event notifications, SNS and EventBridge (scheduled or rule-based). Also CloudWatch Logs, SES and CodeCommit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0101","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"What is an ESM, and which services use it?","a":"<p>An ESM is a Lambda-managed resource that polls a stream or queue and invokes your function with batches. It is used by SQS, Kinesis Data Streams, DynamoDB Streams, Amazon MSK or self-managed Kafka, and Amazon MQ. Lambda owns the polling, batching, checkpointing and scaling of pollers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0102","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"What happens when an asynchronous invocation fails?","a":"<p>Lambda retries automatically twice (3 attempts in total) with delays. If it still fails, the event goes to the configured <strong>Dead Letter Queue</strong> (SQS or SNS) or <strong>On-failure Destination</strong>. Tune <code>MaximumRetryAttempts</code> (0 to 2) and <code>MaximumEventAge</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-event-invoke-config --function-name f \\\n  --maximum-retry-attempts 1 --maximum-event-age-in-seconds 3600 \\\n  --destination-config &#x27;{&quot;OnFailure&quot;:{&quot;Destination&quot;:&quot;arn:aws:sqs:...:dlq&quot;}}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0103","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"DLQ vs Lambda Destinations?","a":"<p>A DLQ (SQS or SNS) captures only the <strong>failed event payload</strong> after async retries run out. <strong>Destinations</strong> route on <strong>success or failure</strong> and send full context (request plus response or error) to SQS, SNS, EventBridge or another Lambda. They are the modern, more informative option for async invocations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0104","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"When use Destinations over SQS/SNS DLQs?","a":"<p>Prefer Destinations when you want success routing, full invocation context (not just the payload) or workflow chaining. Use a plain DLQ when you only need failed payloads for later reprocessing and want the simplest setup. A DLQ applies to async invocations and to ESM sources. Destinations apply to async invocations and to stream ESMs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0105","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does Lambda poll SQS, and what are batch size vs batch window?","a":"<p>Lambda runs pollers that call <code>ReceiveMessage</code> and invoke your function with a batch. <strong>Batch size</strong> is the maximum records per invocation (up to 10,000 for standard SQS, within a 6 MB payload). <strong>Batch window</strong> (<code>MaximumBatchingWindowInSeconds</code>, 0 to 300) is how long Lambda waits to gather a fuller batch before invoking. Successful batches are deleted from the queue automatically. Failures return to the queue after the visibility timeout.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  FromQueue:\n    Type: SQS\n    Properties:\n      Queue: !GetAtt MyQueue.Arn\n      BatchSize: 10\n      MaximumBatchingWindowInSeconds: 20\n      FunctionResponseTypes: [ReportBatchItemFailures]   # partial batch success</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0106","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does Maximum Concurrency work with SQS as an event source?","a":"<p><code>MaximumConcurrency</code> (2 to 1000) on the SQS ESM caps how many concurrent invocations the poller drives. It protects downstream systems and stops a busy queue from consuming all account concurrency, without reserved concurrency (which throttled the pollers awkwardly).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  FromQueue:\n    Type: SQS\n    Properties:\n      Queue: !GetAtt MyQueue.Arn\n      ScalingConfig: { MaximumConcurrency: 50 }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0107","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"How does Lambda handle Kinesis or DynamoDB Streams?","a":"<p>Lambda reads records per shard in order, one batch at a time, and checkpoints on success. Concurrency is tied to shards: by default one batch per shard at a time, and <code>ParallelizationFactor</code> (up to 10) allows several concurrent batches per shard while keeping per-partition-key order. A failing batch blocks that shard until it succeeds or is discarded under your error-handling config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0108","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"How does Lambda handle poison pill records in stream processing?","a":"<p>A poison pill is a record that always fails and blocks the shard. Mitigations: set <code>MaximumRetryAttempts</code> and <code>MaximumRecordAgeInSeconds</code> so it is eventually discarded, enable <code>BisectBatchOnFunctionError</code> to isolate it, and configure an <strong>on-failure destination</strong> to capture discarded records instead of losing them.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0109","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"What is Bisect on Function Error?","a":"<p><code>BisectBatchOnFunctionError</code> splits a failing batch in half and retries each half, narrowing down until it isolates the single offending record. Good records succeed while only the poison record is retried or discarded, instead of failing the whole batch.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0110","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"API Gateway Proxy Integration vs Custom Integration?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Proxy (Lambda proxy) integration</strong>: API Gateway passes the entire raw request (path, headers, query, body) to Lambda as the standard <code>event</code>, and Lambda must return a <code>{statusCode, headers, body}</code> shape. Simple, flexible, minimal mapping config.</li>\n  <li><strong>Custom (non-proxy) integration</strong>: you use mapping templates (VTL) to transform the request and response between client and Lambda. More control and decoupling, more configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Proxy integration must return this exact shape\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; ({\n  statusCode: 200,\n  headers: { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot; },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ msg: `Hello ${event.queryStringParameters?.name ?? &quot;world&quot;}` }),\n});</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0111","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"How do you trigger a function on a schedule?","a":"<p>Use Amazon EventBridge (Scheduler or a rule) with a <code>rate()</code> or <code>cron()</code> expression. EventBridge invokes the function asynchronously.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  EveryFiveMinutes:\n    Type: Schedule\n    Properties:\n      Schedule: rate(5 minutes)        # or cron(0 12 * * ? *)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0112","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What is the AWS root user, and how should you treat its credentials?","a":"<p>The root user is created with the account and has full access to everything. Keep its credentials in a secure place (password manager or safe) and use it only for tasks that require it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0113","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What can happen if root credentials are stolen?","a":"<p>An attacker can lock you out and run up charges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0114","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"Which users should you create for daily work, and what do you do if the admin user is compromised?","a":"<p>Create an <strong>IAM admin user</strong> for daily work and limited IAM users for other tasks. If the admin user is compromised, sign in as root, delete that user and create a new one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0115","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What are IAM groups for?","a":"<p>They organize users. Attach permissions to the group and every member inherits them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0116","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What are IAM policies and what elements do they have?","a":"<p>JSON documents with <code>Effect</code> (Allow or Deny), <code>Action</code> (API actions, for example <code>dynamodb:GetItem</code>) and <code>Resource</code> (the ARN). Users and groups have no permissions until a policy is attached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0117","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"Why use fine-grained IAM permissions, and what should you avoid?","a":"<p>Specific access to one table limits damage from stolen credentials. Avoid <code>Action: \"*\"</code> on <code>Resource: \"*\"</code>. Rotate access keys regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0118","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What does a policy that allows reading one DynamoDB table look like?","a":"<p>It allows <code>dynamodb:GetItem</code> on the table ARN:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n      &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;dynamodb:GetItem&quot;,\n      &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:dynamodb:us-east-1:123456789012:table/MyTable&quot;\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0119","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What does the overly broad admin-style IAM policy look like?","a":"<p>It allows every action on every resource, which should be avoided:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;,\n&quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0120","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"Which IAM statement grants read access to S3 objects and bucket listing?","a":"<p>Allow <code>s3:GetObject</code> and <code>s3:ListBucket</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Action&quot;: [\n  &quot;s3:GetObject&quot;,\n  &quot;s3:ListBucket&quot;\n],</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0121","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"How do you create an admin user for Lambda work in the AWS console?","a":"<p>Create a group (for example <code>NewAdmin</code>) with the <code>AdministratorAccess</code> policy. Then create a user (for example <code>LambdaUser</code>) with console access and an auto-created password, and add it to the group. The sign-in URL and temporary password are shown once, so save them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0122","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"How do you get programmatic CLI access for an IAM user?","a":"<p>Open the user's <strong>Security credentials</strong>, create an access key for the CLI, store it securely and run <code>aws configure</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0123","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"Which values does <code>aws configure</code> ask for, and where do the keys end up?","a":"<p>Access key ID, secret access key, default region (such as <code>us-east-1</code>) and output format (<code>json</code>). Keys end up in <code>~/.aws/credentials</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0124","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"Which AWS CLI commands create an admin user and a group, and attach them?","a":"<p>Four commands:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam create-user --user-name MyAdminUser\naws iam attach-user-policy --user-name MyAdminUser --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess\naws iam create-group --group-name Developers\naws iam add-user-to-group --group-name Developers --user-name MyAdminUser</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0125","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"What does the <code>~/.aws/credentials</code> file look like?","a":"<p>A <code>[default]</code> profile with the access key ID and secret:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[default]\naws_access_key_id = &lt;ACCESS_KEY_ID&gt;\naws_secret_access_key = &lt;SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0126","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"What is the execution role, and what is a resource-based policy?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Execution role</strong> (identity-based, the IAM role the function assumes): defines what the function's code may do to <em>other</em> AWS services (read a DynamoDB table, write to S3). This is <em>outbound</em> permission.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource-based policy</strong> (attached to the function): defines <em>who</em> may <em>invoke</em> the function (for example \"allow S3 bucket X to invoke me\"). This is <em>inbound</em> permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: {&quot;Service&quot;: &quot;s3.amazonaws.com&quot;},\n  &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:...:function:f&quot;,\n  &quot;Condition&quot;: {&quot;ArnLike&quot;: {&quot;AWS:SourceArn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket&quot;}} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0127","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"How do you give a function access to an S3 bucket?","a":"<p>Attach an IAM policy to the function's <strong>execution role</strong> that grants the needed S3 actions on the specific bucket or prefix (least privilege).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;, &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;, &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;],\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot;\n}]}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0128","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"What is least privilege for Lambda IAM roles?","a":"<p>Grant the execution role only the exact actions on the exact resources the function needs. No <code>Action: \"*\"</code> or <code>Resource: \"*\"</code>. Scope to specific ARNs and prefixes and use conditions. This limits the blast radius if the function or its credentials are compromised.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0129","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"How should sensitive credentials or API keys reach a function?","a":"<p>Never hard-code them. Store them in <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong> or <strong>SSM Parameter Store (SecureString)</strong>, grant the execution role read access, and fetch at Init (cache in a global). The Secrets/Parameter Store Lambda extension can cache values and reduce API calls.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3, os\n_secret = None\ndef get_secret():\n    global _secret\n    if _secret is None:                      # fetch once per environment\n        _secret = boto3.client(&quot;secretsmanager&quot;) \\\n            .get_secret_value(SecretId=os.environ[&quot;SECRET_ARN&quot;])[&quot;SecretString&quot;]\n    return _secret</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0130","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"Environment variables vs Secrets Manager / SSM Parameter Store?","a":"<p>Env vars are simple key/values stored with the function config. They suit non-secret config (table names, flags) and are encrypted at rest with KMS. Secrets Manager and SSM are built for secrets: centralized, access-controlled, auditable and they support <strong>rotation</strong>. Sensitive values belong there, not in env vars, which are visible to anyone with <code>GetFunctionConfiguration</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0131","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"How do you encrypt env vars at rest and in transit?","a":"<p>At rest Lambda encrypts them with a KMS key (AWS-managed by default; use a customer-managed CMK for tighter control and audit). For transit and console protection, enable <strong>encryption helpers</strong> so values are encrypted client-side with your CMK and decrypted in code at runtime via KMS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0132","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"How do Lambda Authorizers work with API Gateway?","a":"<p>A Lambda Authorizer is a function API Gateway calls before the target function to authenticate and authorize a request. <strong>Token</strong> authorizers inspect a bearer token. <strong>Request</strong> authorizers inspect headers, query and context. It returns an IAM policy (Allow or Deny) plus optional context, and API Gateway caches the result by key and TTL.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; ({\n  principalId: &quot;user123&quot;,\n  policyDocument: { Version: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;, Statement: [{\n    Action: &quot;execute-api:Invoke&quot;,\n    Effect: isValid(event.authorizationToken) ? &quot;Allow&quot; : &quot;Deny&quot;,\n    Resource: event.methodArn }] },\n  context: { role: &quot;admin&quot; },   // passed to the backend function\n});</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0133","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"How do you stop an arbitrary S3 bucket from triggering your function?","a":"<p>On the function's <strong>resource-based policy</strong>, scope the S3 principal with a <code>SourceArn</code> condition (the specific bucket ARN) and <code>SourceAccount</code>. Only that bucket can invoke the function, which prevents a confused-deputy case where another bucket or account triggers it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0134","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"Can a function in Account A be triggered by an S3 event in Account B?","a":"<p>Yes. Add a resource-based policy on the Account A function that allows the S3 service principal with <code>SourceAccount</code> = Account B and <code>SourceArn</code> = the Account B bucket. Configure the Account B bucket notification to point at the Account A function ARN. Cross-account access needs explicit permissions on both sides.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0135","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"How do code signing and AWS Signer prevent unauthorized code?","a":"<p>With <strong>Code Signing for Lambda</strong> you attach a signing profile (AWS Signer). Lambda verifies the package signature against trusted publishers before accepting it, and rejects unsigned, tampered or expired packages. Only trusted, unmodified code gets deployed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0136","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"What is cross-account deployment for Lambda?","a":"<p>Deploying or updating a function's code or infrastructure from another account, for example a central CI/CD account deploying into dev and prod accounts. It is usually done with cross-account IAM roles (the pipeline assumes a role in the target account), shared artifact buckets or ECR with cross-account policies, and CloudFormation/SAM StackSets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0137","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"How do you isolate tenants in a multi-tenant serverless system?","a":"<p>Along a spectrum: a separate AWS account per tenant (strongest isolation), or shared functions with per-tenant IAM scoping using <strong>session tags or dynamic policies</strong> (<code>sts:AssumeRole</code> with tenant context, ABAC conditions on <code>${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant}</code>), per-tenant resource prefixes or partition keys, and reserved concurrency per tenant to prevent noisy neighbors. Choose by isolation against cost and operational overhead.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0138","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"What happens when a function is attached to a VPC?","a":"<p>The function can reach private VPC resources (RDS, ElastiCache, internal services) through Elastic Network Interfaces. It then follows VPC routing, so it loses default internet access unless you add a NAT gateway (private subnets) or a VPC endpoint. Thanks to AWS Hyperplane, ENIs are shared and set up at function create or update time, so VPC attachment no longer adds a per-invoke cold-start penalty.</p>\n<p>Without a VPC, functions run in an AWS-managed network with internet access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0139","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"What is an ENI, and how does Hyperplane optimize VPC access?","a":"<p>An ENI is a virtual network card that gives the function an IP inside your VPC. Historically Lambda created an ENI per concurrent environment (slow and IP-hungry). <strong>Hyperplane</strong> now creates shared, network-mapped ENIs per function, subnet and security-group combination at create or update time and multiplexes many execution environments through them. That removes per-invocation ENI setup and its cold-start cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0140","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"How can a VPC-bound function reach the public internet?","a":"<p>Place the function in <strong>private</strong> subnets and route <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> through a <strong>NAT gateway</strong> in a public subnet, which reaches the internet via an Internet Gateway. A function in a public subnet cannot reach the internet directly because it has no public IP. For AWS services without internet, use VPC endpoints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0141","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"How do VPC Endpoints (PrivateLink) let a private function reach AWS services?","a":"<p>VPC endpoints keep traffic on the AWS private network. <strong>Gateway endpoints</strong> (S3, DynamoDB) add a route-table entry. <strong>Interface endpoints (PrivateLink)</strong> create an ENI with a private IP for services such as Secrets Manager, SQS and KMS. A private, no-NAT function can then reach those services securely and often more cheaply.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0142","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What is the default regional concurrency limit in a new account?","a":"<p>1,000 concurrent executions per region (a soft limit, raised through a Service Quotas request), shared across all functions in that region and account.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0143","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"Reserved Concurrency vs Provisioned Concurrency?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Reserved Concurrency</strong> carves out a guaranteed slice of the account limit for one function and also caps that function at that number. It manages <em>how much</em> concurrency a function can use, at no extra cost.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provisioned Concurrency</strong> pre-warms N environments so invocations skip cold starts. It manages <em>latency and readiness</em>, and you pay for the pre-warmed capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name f --reserved-concurrent-executions 100</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0144","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What happens when a function exceeds its concurrency limit?","a":"<p>Lambda throttles: it rejects extra concurrent invocations with a <code>429 TooManyRequestsException</code> (<code>ThrottleReason</code>). What happens next depends on the invocation type.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0145","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"How do sync calls react to throttling vs async calls?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Synchronous</strong>: the caller immediately gets a <code>429</code> and must handle or retry it (API Gateway surfaces a 5xx or 429 to the client).</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous</strong>: Lambda keeps the event in its internal queue and retries for up to about 6 hours with backoff. If it never succeeds, the event goes to the DLQ or on-failure destination. Async throttling is largely absorbed, while sync throttling is felt by the caller.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0146","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What status code does a throttled synchronous API Gateway request return?","a":"<p>Lambda returns <code>429 TooManyRequestsException</code>. Depending on integration config, API Gateway may show the client a <code>429</code>, or a <code>502</code>/<code>500</code> if not mapped. The Lambda-level reason is a throttle.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0147","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How is total concurrency computed?","a":"<p>Concurrency = requests per second x average duration in seconds. For example 100 req/s x 0.2 s = 20 concurrent executions. This (Little's Law) tells you how much to reserve or provision and whether you will approach the limit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0148","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"What is burst concurrency, and how does scaling work beyond it?","a":"<p>On a sudden spike Lambda provides an initial burst of environments instantly, then scales at a steady rate (for example +500 environments per function per minute in supported regions) until demand is met or the limit is reached. During ramp-up, requests beyond available concurrency are throttled (sync) or queued (async).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0149","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How do you monitor concurrency across a region?","a":"<p>Watch the CloudWatch <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code> metric (account-wide and per function) and <code>UnreservedConcurrentExecutions</code>. Alarm as you approach the account limit, and use <code>ClaimedAccountConcurrency</code> plus the Lambda console and Service Quotas to track headroom.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0150","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"How does Reserved Concurrency protect downstream resources such as databases?","a":"<p>Capping a function's maximum concurrency limits how many simultaneous connections it can open to a fragile downstream, such as an RDS instance with few connections. This stops Lambda's rapid scaling from overwhelming the database. Pair it with RDS Proxy for pooling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0151","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"What if one function monopolizes all unreserved concurrency?","a":"<p>Other functions get throttled because they compete for the same shared pool. Set <strong>reserved concurrency</strong> on high-traffic functions (to cap them) and reserve concurrency for critical functions so they always have headroom. For example, with a 1,000 pool: a critical function reserved 200 (guaranteed and capped), a batch function reserved 100, and the remaining 700 unreserved are shared by everything else.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0152","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"How do you prevent runaway cost from recursive loops?","a":"<p>Lambda has built-in <strong>recursive loop detection</strong> (for example Lambda to SQS/SNS to the same Lambda) that stops the loop and notifies you. Also set <strong>reserved concurrency</strong> to cap the blast radius, add CloudWatch billing and concurrency alarms, avoid a function writing to its own trigger source, and use idempotency plus circuit breakers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Cap a function so a runaway loop can&#x27;t consume the whole account limit\naws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name f --reserved-concurrent-executions 10</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0153","card":"q-lambda-039-soft-limits-vs-hard-limits","sec":"lambda-s040","q":"What are soft limits vs hard limits?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Soft limits (quotas)</strong>: adjustable through Service Quotas, for example the default 1,000 concurrency and function or layer storage.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hard limits</strong>: fixed by the platform, for example the 15-min timeout, 6 MB sync and 256 KB async payload, 10 GB memory, 10 GB <code>/tmp</code>, 250 MB unzipped zip and 512 KB total env var size. Design around hard limits and request increases for soft ones.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0154","card":"q-lambda-040-stateless-design-and-where-to-keep-state","sec":"lambda-s041","q":"Why is Lambda stateless, and where should application state live?","a":"<p>Each execution environment is ephemeral and any invocation may land on a fresh one, so nothing in memory or <code>/tmp</code> is guaranteed to persist. Store state externally: <strong>DynamoDB</strong> (key/value, sessions, counters, idempotency), <strong>S3</strong> (objects and files), <strong>RDS/Aurora</strong> (relational) and <strong>ElastiCache</strong> (fast shared cache). Warm-environment caching is a performance optimization, not a correctness guarantee.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Persistence</th><th>Scope</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>/tmp</code> (512 MB to 10 GB)</td><td>ephemeral</td><td>single environment</td><td>scratch files during processing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>EFS</td><td>durable</td><td>shared, many functions</td><td>large models, shared file system, POSIX (needs VPC)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>S3</td><td>durable</td><td>global</td><td>inputs and outputs, blobs, artifacts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DynamoDB</td><td>durable</td><td>global</td><td>sessions, counters, key/value, idempotency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>RDS/Aurora + RDS Proxy</td><td>durable</td><td>shared</td><td>relational data (proxy pools connections)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ElastiCache</td><td>in-memory</td><td>shared</td><td>hot cache, fast shared state</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0155","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"How do you attach an EFS volume to a function?","a":"<p>Put the function in the same VPC as the EFS mount target, create an <strong>EFS Access Point</strong>, and set the function's <code>FileSystemConfig</code> to mount it at a local path (for example <code>/mnt/data</code>). The execution role needs EFS permissions. The function then reads and writes EFS like a shared, durable local file system.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    VpcConfig: { SubnetIds: [...], SecurityGroupIds: [...] }\n    FileSystemConfigs:\n      - Arn: !GetAtt AccessPoint.Arn\n        LocalMountPath: /mnt/data       # must start with /mnt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0156","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"When use <code>/tmp</code>, EFS or S3?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>/tmp</code></strong> (512 MB to 10 GB): fast, local, ephemeral scratch space for one invocation or warm environment.</li>\n  <li><strong>EFS</strong>: a shared, durable, low-latency POSIX file system across many concurrent functions. Use for large models, shared state, or when you need a file system. Requires VPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>S3</strong>: durable, massively scalable object storage for inputs, outputs, artifacts and large blobs accessed over the API (no VPC needed).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0157","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"How does Step Functions orchestrate stateful workflows with Lambda?","a":"<p>Step Functions is a managed state machine that coordinates several Lambdas (and other services) with sequencing, branching (<code>Choice</code>), parallelism (<code>Map</code>/<code>Parallel</code>), retries, error handling and wait states. The workflow state is external, so each Lambda stays stateless and short. It suits long-running, multi-step processes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;ResizeImage&quot;: {\n    &quot;Type&quot;: &quot;Task&quot;,\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:...:function:resize&quot;,\n    &quot;Retry&quot;: [{ &quot;ErrorEquals&quot;: [&quot;States.TaskFailed&quot;], &quot;MaxAttempts&quot;: 2 }],\n    &quot;Next&quot;: &quot;Notify&quot; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0158","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"What is the max payload Step Functions passes between Lambda tasks?","a":"<p>256 KB per state transition. For larger data pass a <strong>reference</strong> (for example an S3 key) and have the Lambdas read and write the real payload in S3.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0159","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"How do you manage DB connection pooling with RDS Proxy and Lambda?","a":"<p>Lambda's rapid scaling can exhaust database connections. <strong>RDS Proxy</strong> sits between Lambda and RDS or Aurora, keeps a warm pool and multiplexes many Lambda connections onto fewer DB connections. Lambda connects to the proxy endpoint (IAM auth supported), which smooths spikes and improves failover. Pair it with reserved concurrency.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Connect to RDS through RDS Proxy (endpoint = proxy, not the DB directly)\nimport os, pymysql\nconn = pymysql.connect(host=os.environ[&quot;PROXY_ENDPOINT&quot;],  # created outside handler\n                       user=os.environ[&quot;DB_USER&quot;], db=&quot;app&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.execute(&quot;SELECT 1&quot;)\n        return cur.fetchone()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0160","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"How does Lambda integrate with DynamoDB Streams for change data capture?","a":"<p>Enable a stream on the table. An <strong>event source mapping</strong> polls it and invokes Lambda with ordered batches of change records (<code>INSERT</code>/<code>MODIFY</code>/<code>REMOVE</code>, with old and new images). Use it for CDC, replication, aggregations, notifications and materialized views.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    for r in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        if r[&quot;eventName&quot;] == &quot;INSERT&quot;:\n            new_item = r[&quot;dynamodb&quot;][&quot;NewImage&quot;]\n            process(new_item)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0161","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What is Lambda@Edge, and how does it differ from standard Lambda?","a":"<p>Lambda@Edge runs functions at CloudFront edge locations, close to users, on CloudFront events (viewer or origin request and response). It customizes or inspects HTTP at the edge (redirects, header changes, A/B tests, auth). Compared with standard Lambda it is replicated globally to the edge, has tighter limits (size, timeout, no VPC or env vars) and supports only Node.js and Python.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0162","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What are CloudFront Functions, and how do they compare with Lambda@Edge?","a":"<p>CloudFront Functions are very lightweight JavaScript functions that run at the edge on viewer request and response, with sub-millisecond startup, massive scale and very low cost, but tight constraints (very short runtime, tiny memory, no network or file access). Use them for simple high-volume tasks (header rewrites, redirects, token checks). Use <strong>Lambda@Edge</strong> when you need more compute, longer execution, network calls or origin-event access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0163","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"How do Lambda Extensions work, and what are internal vs external extensions?","a":"<p>Extensions plug tools (observability, security, config) into the Lambda lifecycle through the Extensions API. <strong>Internal extensions</strong> run in-process inside the runtime (wrapper scripts, language agents). <strong>External extensions</strong> run as separate processes alongside the function, can start during Init and keep running through Invoke and Shutdown. They suit shipping logs and metrics and caching secrets without blocking the handler.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0164","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"What are Lambda Layers, and how do they manage shared code?","a":"<p>A Layer is a <code>.zip</code> archive of libraries, custom runtimes or shared code that you attach to functions. It is extracted into <code>/opt</code> at runtime. Layers let several functions share dependencies without bundling them into each package: smaller deployment packages, faster deploys and central versioning.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyLayer:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::LayerVersion\n  Properties:\n    ContentUri: dependencies/         # e.g. python/lib/... structure\n    CompatibleRuntimes: [python3.12]\nMyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Layers: [!Ref MyLayer]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0165","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"What are the limits on Layers?","a":"<p>Up to <strong>5 layers</strong> per function. The <strong>combined unzipped size</strong> of function code and all layers must be at most <strong>250 MB</strong>. Layers count toward the same limit, so they save package size but not the total footprint.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0166","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"What does CloudWatch collect, and does Lambda need code changes to send metrics?","a":"<p>CloudWatch collects logs, metrics and events from AWS services. Lambda sends metrics automatically with no code change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0167","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"Which metrics does a function's Monitoring tab show?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Invocations</strong>: how many times the function was triggered. Shows usage patterns and traffic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Duration</strong>: minimum, average and maximum time per request. Shows variation and where to optimize.</li>\n  <li><strong>Error count and success rate</strong>: a 100% success rate with no errors means a healthy function.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0168","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"Which alarms can you configure on Lambda metrics, and what does duration data guide?","a":"<p>Alarms on high error rate or long duration. Duration data guides memory tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0169","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"How do logs get from a Lambda function to CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>Lambda sends anything written with <code>console.log</code> (or the language equivalent) to CloudWatch Logs automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0170","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"How are Lambda logs structured in CloudWatch?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>A <strong>log group</strong> per function (<code>/aws/lambda/my-lambda-function</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Log streams</strong> inside it (created on deploy, over time, or when a stream fills).</li>\n  <li><strong>Log events</strong> as entries.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0171","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"Which lines does Lambda write into the logs itself, and what does <code>REPORT</code> show?","a":"<p><code>START</code>, <code>END</code> and <code>REPORT</code>. <code>REPORT</code> shows duration, billed duration, memory size, max memory used and init duration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0172","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"How long are CloudWatch Logs kept by default, and what should you do about it?","a":"<p>They are stored indefinitely by default, so set a retention policy to control cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0173","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"What logging advice applies to Lambda functions?","a":"<p>Log sparingly and log JSON (<code>JSON.stringify(event)</code>) for complex data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0174","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"Where can you view Lambda logs?","a":"<p>In the Monitoring tab (filter by time, auto-refresh) or in the CloudWatch console.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0175","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"Where are Lambda execution logs stored by default?","a":"<p>In <strong>Amazon CloudWatch Logs</strong>, in a log group named <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code>, split into log streams per execution environment. Anything printed (<code>console.log</code>, <code>print</code>, stdout and stderr) plus the automatic <code>START</code>/<code>END</code>/<code>REPORT</code> lines land there. The execution role needs <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0176","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"Which CloudWatch metrics does Lambda publish automatically?","a":"<p><code>Invocations</code>, <code>Errors</code>, <code>Duration</code>, <code>Throttles</code>, <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code>, <code>DeadLetterErrors</code>, <code>IteratorAge</code> (streams), <code>ProvisionedConcurrency*</code> metrics and <code>Async*</code> event metrics. From them you derive error rate, latency percentiles and concurrency headroom.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0177","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"What is AWS X-Ray, and how do you trace a function?","a":"<p>X-Ray is a distributed tracing service. Enable <strong>Active Tracing</strong> on the function. Lambda emits a trace segment per invocation, and the X-Ray SDK auto-instruments downstream calls (DynamoDB, HTTP and so on) as subsegments. You see the end-to-end request path and latency bottlenecks and errors on a service map.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from aws_xray_sdk.core import xray_recorder, patch_all\npatch_all()   # auto-trace boto3 / requests calls\n\n@xray_recorder.capture(&quot;business_logic&quot;)\ndef do_work(event):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0178","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"How do you compute total billable duration from <code>REPORT</code> logs?","a":"<p>Each <code>REPORT</code> line shows <code>Billed Duration</code> (ms, rounded up to the nearest 1 ms) and <code>Memory Size</code>. Cost is about the sum of (Billed Duration in seconds x Memory in GB) x price per GB-second, plus the per-request cost. Sum <code>Billed Duration</code> across invocations (or use CloudWatch <code>Duration</code> x count) to estimate billable time.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>REPORT RequestId: ...  Duration: 123.45 ms  Billed Duration: 124 ms  Memory Size: 128 MB  Max Memory Used: 50 MB</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0179","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"How do you write structured JSON logs for aggregators?","a":"<p>Emit single-line JSON so CloudWatch Logs Insights and aggregators (Datadog, ELK) can parse fields. Set the function's log format to <strong>JSON</strong> (advanced logging controls) and/or log JSON yourself with a request id or correlation id.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json, logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(); log.setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    log.info(json.dumps({\n        &quot;level&quot;: &quot;INFO&quot;, &quot;requestId&quot;: context.aws_request_id,\n        &quot;action&quot;: &quot;order.created&quot;, &quot;orderId&quot;: event.get(&quot;id&quot;)}))</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0180","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"What is the <code>context</code> object, and what does it provide?","a":"<p>Runtime information about the invocation: <code>aws_request_id</code>, <code>function_name</code>, <code>function_version</code>, <code>memory_limit_in_mb</code>, <code>log_group_name</code>/<code>log_stream_name</code>, <code>invoked_function_arn</code> and <code>get_remaining_time_in_millis()</code> (time left before timeout, useful to bail out or checkpoint).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if context.get_remaining_time_in_millis() &lt; 2000:\n        return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;deferred&quot;}      # not enough time left\n    return process(event)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0181","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do you unit test Lambda functions without calling AWS?","a":"<p>Keep business logic separate from the handler and test it directly. Mock AWS SDK calls with <strong>moto</strong> (Python) or <strong>aws-sdk-client-mock</strong> (JS), and pass a fake <code>event</code> and <code>context</code>. This runs fast in CI with no real AWS calls.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># test_handler.py: moto mocks DynamoDB, no real AWS\nfrom moto import mock_aws\nimport boto3, app\n\n@mock_aws\ndef test_handler():\n    boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;).create_table(...)   # in-memory mock\n    resp = app.lambda_handler({&quot;id&quot;: &quot;1&quot;}, None)\n    assert resp[&quot;statusCode&quot;] == 200</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0182","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"What local emulation tools exist (SAM CLI, LocalStack)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>AWS SAM CLI</strong>: <code>sam local invoke</code> and <code>sam local start-api</code> run the function or API in a Docker container that emulates the Lambda runtime.</li>\n  <li><strong>LocalStack</strong>: emulates many AWS services (S3, DynamoDB, SQS, Lambda) locally for integration tests.</li>\n  <li>Also <code>serverless-offline</code> and direct handler calls in unit tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam local invoke MyFunction -e events/event.json\nsam local start-api        # serves the API at http://localhost:3000</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0183","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do you handle unhandled exceptions or crashes?","a":"<p>Unhandled exceptions are logged to CloudWatch with the stack trace and increment the <code>Errors</code> metric. For sync calls the error returns to the caller. For async calls it triggers retries, then the DLQ or destination. Best practice: wrap handler logic in try/except, log structured errors, and let genuinely retryable failures throw so Lambda's retry and DLQ machinery engages.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        return process(event)\n    except RetryableError:\n        raise                      # let Lambda retry / send to DLQ\n    except Exception as e:\n        log.exception(&quot;unrecoverable&quot;)   # stack trace to CloudWatch\n        return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 500}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0184","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do CloudWatch Logs Insights queries help diagnose latency or failures?","a":"<p>Logs Insights queries log groups with a purpose-built language to filter, aggregate and sort: find the slowest invocations, count errors or extract fields from JSON logs, far faster than scrolling streams.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @timestamp, @duration, @message\n| filter @type = &quot;REPORT&quot;\n| sort @duration desc\n| limit 20</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0185","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What problems come from building serverless apps in the console?","a":"<p>It is error-prone, tedious, hard to maintain and impossible to replicate across test, dev and production accounts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0186","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?","a":"<p>Defining infrastructure in YAML or JSON and treating it like application code (testing, code review, version control).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0187","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What are the advantages of IaC?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Programmatic management</strong>: no manual console clicks, fewer human errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Replicability</strong>: reuse the same scripts across environments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Version control</strong>: Git records who changed what and why.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scalability</strong>: one engineer can manage large infrastructure, with reusable components.</li>\n  <li><strong>Quality and security</strong>: code review and automated tests apply to infrastructure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Speed</strong>: reuse existing templates.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0188","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"How do you put a template under version control with Git?","a":"<p>Add and commit the file:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>git add template.yaml\ngit commit -m &quot;Added Lambda function definition&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0189","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"What is AWS SAM?","a":"<p>The Serverless Application Model: an open-source framework for building serverless applications, built on CloudFormation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0190","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"How does SAM extend CloudFormation?","a":"<p>It offers a shorthand syntax. <code>Transform: AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31</code> expands concise resources such as <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code>, <code>Api</code> and <code>Table</code> into full CloudFormation at deploy time. You can still drop to raw CloudFormation for anything SAM does not cover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0191","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"What are the two components of SAM?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><strong>SAM transform templates</strong>: YAML with simplified syntax for Lambda, API Gateway and DynamoDB.</li>\n  <li><strong>SAM CLI</strong>: deploys templates, tests locally, integrates with CI/CD.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0192","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"What does the SAM CLI add?","a":"<p><code>build</code>, local testing (<code>local invoke</code>, <code>start-api</code>), guided <code>deploy</code> and <code>logs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0193","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"What are SAM Local, SAM Accelerate and the CI/CD integration?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SAM Local</strong>: test and debug locally, emulating Lambda and API Gateway.</li>\n  <li><strong>SAM Accelerate</strong>: faster development with <code>sam sync</code>, parallel updates without full redeploys and aggregated logs.</li>\n  <li><strong>CI/CD integration</strong>: CodeBuild, CodeDeploy and CodePipeline, with <code>sam pipeline init</code>.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0194","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"What does a minimal SAM function with an API event look like?","a":"<p>An <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code> with handler, runtime, <code>CodeUri</code> and an <code>Api</code> event:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Resources:\n  MyLambdaFunction:\n    Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n    Properties:\n      Handler: index.handler\n      Runtime: nodejs18.x\n      CodeUri: .\n      Events:\n        ApiEvent:\n          Type: Api\n          Properties:\n            Path: /hello\n            Method: GET</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0195","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"Which SAM CLI commands deploy guided, invoke locally, sync and start a pipeline?","a":"<p>Four commands:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam deploy --guided\nsam local invoke MyLambdaFunction -e event.json\nsam sync --stack-name my-stack\nsam pipeline init</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0196","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"How do you define an explicit API Gateway API in a SAM template?","a":"<p>Use an <code>AWS::Serverless::Api</code> resource with <code>StageName</code> and a <code>DefinitionBody</code> (Swagger 2.0), then point the function's <code>Api</code> event at it with <code>RestApiId: !Ref MyApi</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyApi:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Api\n  Properties:\n    StageName: prod</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0197","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"How does a SAM function event attach to an explicit API?","a":"<p>With <code>RestApiId: !Ref MyApi</code> plus the <code>Path</code> and <code>Method</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  ApiEvent:\n    Type: Api\n    Properties:\n      RestApiId: !Ref MyApi\n      Path: /hello\n      Method: GET</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0198","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"How does the Swagger definition integrate <code>/hello</code> with the Lambda function in SAM?","a":"<p>With <code>x-amazon-apigateway-integration</code>, a Lambda invocation <code>uri</code> built with <code>Fn::Sub</code>, and <code>httpMethod: POST</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x-amazon-apigateway-integration:\n  uri:\n    Fn::Sub: arn:aws:apigateway:${AWS::Region}:lambda:path/2015-03-31/functions/${MyLambdaFunction.Arn}/invocations\n  httpMethod: POST</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0199","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"What are the first two lines of a SAM template?","a":"<p>The template format version and the SAM transform:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>AWSTemplateFormatVersion: &#x27;2010-09-09&#x27;\nTransform: AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0200","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"What are the prerequisites for installing AWS SAM?","a":"<p>An AWS account with an IAM admin user (access key and secret), the AWS CLI configured with <code>aws configure</code>, and Docker (SAM Local needs it running).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0201","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"How do you install the SAM CLI on each operating system?","a":"<p>Windows: MSI or package manager. macOS: Homebrew. Linux: package manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0202","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"Which commands check prerequisites and install the SAM CLI with Homebrew?","a":"<p>Check versions, then tap and install:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws --version\ndocker --version\nbrew tap aws/tap\nbrew install aws-sam-cli\nsam --version</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0203","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"Which choices do you make in <code>sam init</code> for the Hello World project?","a":"<p><strong>AWS Quick Start Templates</strong>, the <strong>Hello World Example</strong>, a runtime (Node.js 18 or Python), package type <strong>Zip</strong>, language or dependency manager (<code>pip</code> for Python), X-Ray tracing <strong>No</strong>, Application Insights <strong>No</strong>, and a project name (<code>sam-first-project</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0204","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"Which key files does a new SAM project have?","a":"<p><code>README.md</code> (setup, testing and deployment), <code>template.yaml</code> (resources), and the function folder (<code>hello-world/app.js</code>, or <code>hello_world/app.py</code> plus <code>requirements.txt</code> for Python).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0205","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"Which commands create a SAM project and look inside it?","a":"<p><code>sam init</code>, then change into the project and list it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam init\ncd sam-first-project\nls</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0206","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"What sections does a SAM <code>template.yaml</code> have?","a":"<p>A Description, <strong>Globals</strong> (for example function timeout 3 s), <strong>Resources</strong> (the function and its API Gateway trigger) and <strong>Outputs</strong> (API endpoint URL, function ARN, implicit IAM role ARN).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0207","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"How do you set a default timeout for all functions in a SAM template?","a":"<p>In the <code>Globals</code> section under <code>Function</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Globals:\n  Function:\n    Timeout: 3</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0208","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"How is the Hello World function defined in <code>template.yaml</code>?","a":"<p>As <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code> with <code>CodeUri: hello-world/</code>, <code>Handler: app.lambdaHandler</code>, runtime <code>nodejs18.x</code>, architecture <code>x86_64</code> and an <code>Api</code> event on <code>/hello</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>HelloWorldFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    CodeUri: hello-world/\n    Handler: app.lambdaHandler\n    Runtime: nodejs18.x\n    Architectures:\n      - x86_64\n    Events:\n      HelloWorld:\n        Type: Api\n        Properties:\n          Path: /hello\n          Method: get</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0209","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"How does a SAM template output the API endpoint URL?","a":"<p>With an <code>Outputs</code> entry using <code>Fn::Sub</code> on <code>${ServerlessRestApi}</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Fn::Sub: https://${ServerlessRestApi}.execute-api.${AWS::Region}.amazonaws.com/Prod/hello/</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0210","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"How do SAM outputs return the function ARN and implicit IAM role ARN?","a":"<p>With <code>Fn::GetAtt</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Fn::GetAtt: HelloWorldFunction.Arn\nFn::GetAtt: HelloWorldFunctionRole.Arn</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0211","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"What does the Node.js Hello World SAM handler look like?","a":"<p>An exported <code>lambdaHandler</code> that returns status 200 and a JSON message:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.lambdaHandler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    const response = {\n        statusCode: 200,\n        body: JSON.stringify({\n            message: &#x27;Hello from Lambda!&#x27;\n        })\n    };\n    return response;\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0212","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"What changes in the SAM template for a Python project?","a":"<p>Use <code>CodeUri: hello_world/</code>, <code>Handler: app.lambda_handler</code> and <code>Runtime: python3.9</code> (or another supported Python version). The handler lives in <code>hello_world/app.py</code>, referenced as <code>app.lambda_handler</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0213","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"What does the Python Hello World SAM handler look like?","a":"<p>A <code>lambda_handler</code> returning status 200 and a JSON message:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return {\n        &#x27;statusCode&#x27;: 200,\n        &#x27;body&#x27;: json.dumps({\n            &#x27;message&#x27;: &#x27;Hello from Lambda!&#x27;\n        })\n    }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0214","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"What does <code>requirements.txt</code> in a SAM Python project hold?","a":"<p>The dependencies. It is empty by default. A listed dependency looks like:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>boto3==1.26.0</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0215","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"Why does SAM suit Lambda, and when is Terraform better?","a":"<p>SAM has a short serverless-focused YAML syntax, built-in local testing and tight AWS integration. Terraform is better for multi-cloud or complex infrastructure but needs more detailed configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0216","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"What does <code>sam build</code> do?","a":"<p>It copies the source files into <code>.aws-sam</code>, installs dependencies, leaves out test code and dev dependencies, and writes a transformed <code>template.yaml</code>. Docker must be running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0217","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"What does <code>sam local invoke</code> do, and why is the first run slow?","a":"<p>It runs one function with an event file. The first run builds a Docker image, so it is slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0218","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"What does <code>sam local start-api</code> do?","a":"<p>It serves the API at <code>http://localhost:3000</code>, so you can test paths without deploying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0219","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"Which commands build, invoke locally, start the local API and call it?","a":"<p>Four commands:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam build\nsam local invoke HelloWorldFunction -e events/event.json\nsam local start-api\ncurl http://localhost:3000/hello</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0220","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"What does a local SAM GET event file for <code>/hello</code> contain?","a":"<p>The HTTP method, path and an empty query string object:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;httpMethod&quot;: &quot;GET&quot;,\n  &quot;path&quot;: &quot;/hello&quot;,\n  &quot;queryStringParameters&quot;: {}\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0221","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"What do you do after editing a SAM handler to see the new response?","a":"<p>Rebuild and re-invoke to see the new logs and response.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0222","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"How do you add a second function to a SAM project?","a":"<p>Add a resource to <code>template.yaml</code>, create its folder (<code>roll-dice/</code>) and an <code>app.mjs</code> handler (ES modules). SAM creates the API Gateway automatically for simple Api events. Mind YAML indentation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0223","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"How is the roll-dice function declared in the SAM template?","a":"<p>With <code>CodeUri: roll-dice/</code>, <code>Handler: app.lambdaHandler</code>, runtime <code>nodejs18.x</code> and an <code>Api</code> event on <code>/dice</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RollADiceFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    CodeUri: roll-dice/\n    Handler: app.lambdaHandler\n    Runtime: nodejs18.x\n    Events:\n      RollADiceAPI:\n        Type: Api\n        Properties:\n          Path: /dice\n          Method: get</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0224","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"How do you test the dice function locally?","a":"<p>Run <code>sam build</code>, <code>sam local invoke RollADiceFunction -e events/event.json</code>, then <code>sam local start-api</code> and <code>curl http://localhost:3000/dice</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0225","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"How does the roll-dice function compute a roll?","a":"<p><code>Math.floor(Math.random() * sides) + 1</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>function rollDice(sides) {\n    return Math.floor(Math.random() * sides) + 1;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0226","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"How does the roll-dice handler return a result and an error?","a":"<p>Success returns status 200 with <code>{ message: result }</code>. A caught error logs and returns status 500 with <code>Error rolling dice</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return {\n    statusCode: 500,\n    body: JSON.stringify({ message: &#x27;Error rolling dice&#x27; })\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0227","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"Which commands deploy a new SAM project?","a":"<p>Run <code>sam build</code>, then <code>sam deploy --guided</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam build\nsam deploy --guided</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0228","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"Which answers does <code>sam deploy --guided</code> use for the sample project?","a":"<p>Stack name <code>sam-first-project</code>, region <code>eu-west-1</code>, confirm changes before deploy <strong>No</strong>, allow SAM to create IAM roles <strong>Yes</strong>, disable rollback <strong>No</strong>, and accept open APIs (learning only).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0229","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"Where are the guided deploy settings saved, and what does that change?","a":"<p>In <code>samconfig.toml</code>. Later deploys are just <code>sam deploy</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0230","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"What does <code>samconfig.toml</code> contain after a guided deploy?","a":"<p>The stack name, region and capabilities:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">TOML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[default.deploy.parameters]\nstack_name = &quot;sam-first-project&quot;\nregion = &quot;eu-west-1&quot;\ncapabilities = &quot;CAPABILITY_IAM&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0231","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"What does SAM do during a deploy?","a":"<p>It creates an S3 bucket for function code, transforms the template to CloudFormation and deploys through CloudFormation. You can watch events, resources and outputs in the CloudFormation console.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0232","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"How do you test a deployed SAM endpoint, and what should production APIs add?","a":"<p>Use the Lambda console (the API Gateway trigger shows the URL) or Postman. Repeated GETs return random rolls such as <code>{\"message\": 4}</code>. Production APIs should add authentication (Cognito or Lambda authorizers).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0233","card":"q-lambda-059-viewing-logs-with-sam-logs","sec":"lambda-s059","q":"What does <code>sam logs</code> do, and what does <code>--tail</code> add?","a":"<p><code>sam logs</code> reads CloudWatch logs from the terminal. <code>--tail</code> streams new entries while you send requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0234","card":"q-lambda-059-viewing-logs-with-sam-logs","sec":"lambda-s059","q":"Which commands read and tail logs for a SAM function?","a":"<p>Use <code>sam logs</code> with the function name, stack name and region, and add <code>--tail</code> to stream:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam logs --name RollADiceFunction --stack-name sam-first-project --region eu-west-1\nsam logs --name RollADiceFunction --stack-name sam-first-project --region eu-west-1 --tail</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0235","card":"q-lambda-059-viewing-logs-with-sam-logs","sec":"lambda-s059","q":"What must match when you run <code>sam logs</code>?","a":"<p>Function name, stack name and region must match <code>template.yaml</code> and <code>samconfig.toml</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0236","card":"q-lambda-060-challenge-dice-with-a-variable-number-of","sec":"lambda-s060","q":"What does the variable-dice SAM challenge require?","a":"<p>A GET request with an optional <code>sides</code> query parameter rolls a die of that size. It defaults to 6 when the parameter is missing or invalid (<code>?sides=abc</code>, negative values). Add a function with path <code>/roll</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0237","card":"q-lambda-060-challenge-dice-with-a-variable-number-of","sec":"lambda-s060","q":"How does the variable-dice function validate <code>sides</code>?","a":"<p>It defaults to 6, then uses <code>parseInt</code> and accepts only a number greater than 0:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>let sides = 6; // Default to 6-sided dice\nif (queryParams.sides) {\n    const parsedSides = parseInt(queryParams.sides);\n    if (!isNaN(parsedSides) &amp;&amp; parsedSides &gt; 0) {\n        sides = parsedSides;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0238","card":"q-lambda-060-challenge-dice-with-a-variable-number-of","sec":"lambda-s060","q":"How is the variable-dice function declared in the SAM template?","a":"<p>With <code>CodeUri: roll-variable-dice/</code>, <code>Handler: app.lambdaHandler</code>, runtime <code>nodejs18.x</code> and an <code>Api</code> event on <code>/roll</code> with method <code>get</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RollVariableDiceFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    CodeUri: roll-variable-dice/\n    Handler: app.lambdaHandler\n    Runtime: nodejs18.x\n    Events:\n      RollVariableDiceAPI:\n        Type: Api\n        Properties:\n          Path: /roll\n          Method: get</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0239","card":"q-lambda-060-challenge-dice-with-a-variable-number-of","sec":"lambda-s060","q":"How does the variable-dice function return the roll?","a":"<p>As a JSON string <code>The result is ${result}</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return {\n    statusCode: 200,\n    body: JSON.stringify(`The result is ${result}`)\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0240","card":"q-lambda-060-challenge-dice-with-a-variable-number-of","sec":"lambda-s060","q":"How do you call the deployed variable-dice API and tail its logs?","a":"<p>Use <code>curl</code> with <code>?sides=4</code>, then <code>sam logs</code> with <code>--tail</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl https://&lt;api-id&gt;.execute-api.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/Prod/roll?sides=4\nsam logs --name RollVariableDiceFunction --stack-name sam-first-project --region &lt;region&gt; --tail</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0241","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"What does the descriptive-dice solution function return?","a":"<p>\"The result is N. You rolled a dice of S sides.\" It is named <code>RollADiceWithSidesFunction</code> (folder <code>roll-dice-sides/</code>) and uses the same validation as the variable-dice challenge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0242","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"How does the solution build its message?","a":"<p>A template string with the result and the number of sides:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const message = `The result is ${result}. You rolled a dice of ${sides} sides.`;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0243","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"How is the solution function declared in the SAM template?","a":"<p>With <code>CodeUri: roll-dice-sides/</code> and an <code>Api</code> event on <code>/roll</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RollADiceWithSidesFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    CodeUri: roll-dice-sides/</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0244","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"How do you test the solution locally and deploy it?","a":"<p>Run <code>sam build</code> and <code>sam local start-api</code>, call the local URLs, then deploy with <code>sam deploy</code> (the saved <code>samconfig.toml</code> is reused) and watch the CloudFormation stack:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl http://localhost:3000/roll\ncurl http://localhost:3000/roll?sides=4\ncurl http://localhost:3000/roll?sides=20\nsam deploy</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0245","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"Can one API Gateway serve several SAM functions?","a":"<p>Yes. The same API Gateway serves several functions (<code>/hello</code>, <code>/roll</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0246","card":"q-lambda-061-challenge-solution-dice-with-a-descripti","sec":"lambda-s061","q":"How do you tail logs of the solution function?","a":"<p>Run <code>sam logs</code> with the function name, stack name and region, plus <code>--tail</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam logs --name RollADiceWithSidesFunction --stack-name sam-first-project --region eu-west-1 --tail</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0247","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"How do versions and aliases support blue/green or canary deployments?","a":"<p>Publishing a <strong>version</strong> creates an immutable snapshot (<code>$LATEST</code> is mutable, numbered versions are frozen). An <strong>alias</strong> is a named reference (for example <code>PROD</code>) to a version and can split traffic between two versions by weight. That enables blue/green (flip the alias) and canary (shift a small percentage first) without changing callers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda publish-version --function-name f\n# send 10% of PROD traffic to v2, 90% to v1 (canary)\naws lambda update-alias --function-name f --name PROD \\\n  --function-version 1 --routing-config &#x27;{&quot;AdditionalVersionWeights&quot;:{&quot;2&quot;:0.1}}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0248","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"How does CodeDeploy manage weighted alias traffic shifting?","a":"<p>CodeDeploy automates alias shifting with deployment configs such as <strong>Canary</strong> (for example 10% for 5 minutes, then 100%) or <strong>Linear</strong> (for example +10% every minute). It watches CloudWatch alarms during the shift and <strong>rolls back automatically</strong> to the previous version if alarms trip. SAM wires this up with <code>DeploymentPreference</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    AutoPublishAlias: live\n    DeploymentPreference:\n      Type: Canary10Percent5Minutes\n      Alarms: [!Ref ErrorAlarm]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0249","card":"q-lambda-063-image-thumbnails-without-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s063","q":"An image uploaded to S3 must become 3 thumbnails. How do you design this with Lambda without timeouts?","a":"<p>S3 <code>ObjectCreated</code> triggers Lambda <strong>asynchronously</strong>. Generate the three sizes in one invocation if it is quick. If it is heavy, <strong>fan out</strong>: one Lambda publishes 3 messages (SNS, SQS or EventBridge) and a worker Lambda resizes each in parallel. Right-size memory (more memory means more CPU and faster image work), stream to <code>/tmp</code>, and configure a DLQ or destination for failures. Fan-out keeps each invocation far below the timeout and parallelizes the work.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Fan-out: emit one message per target size\nimport boto3, json, os\nsns = boto3.client(&quot;sns&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    key = event[&quot;Records&quot;][0][&quot;s3&quot;][&quot;object&quot;][&quot;key&quot;]\n    for size in (128, 256, 512):\n        sns.publish(TopicArn=os.environ[&quot;TOPIC&quot;],\n                    Message=json.dumps({&quot;key&quot;: key, &quot;size&quot;: size}))</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0250","card":"q-lambda-064-protecting-a-legacy-database-from-spikes","sec":"lambda-s064","q":"API Gateway + Lambda is overloading a legacy database during spikes. How do you decouple and protect it?","a":"<p>Insert a buffer: API Gateway to Lambda writes to <strong>SQS</strong> (or Kinesis) and returns 202 immediately. A <strong>worker Lambda</strong> with an ESM <code>MaximumConcurrency</code> cap (and/or reserved concurrency) drains the queue at a rate the DB can handle. Add <strong>RDS Proxy</strong> for connection pooling. A synchronous spike becomes a smoothed, throttled async flow that shields the database.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0251","card":"q-lambda-065-sqs-messages-processed-more-than-once","sec":"lambda-s065","q":"A Lambda on SQS runs fine but messages process multiple times. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Standard SQS is <strong>at-least-once</strong>, so duplicates are expected. A message also reappears if the visibility timeout expires before processing finishes, or if part of a batch fails and the whole batch returns. Fixes: set <strong>visibility timeout at least 6x the function timeout</strong>, enable <strong>partial batch responses</strong> (<code>ReportBatchItemFailures</code>) so only failed messages retry, make processing <strong>idempotent</strong>, or use a <strong>FIFO queue</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Partial batch response: only failed messages return to the queue\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    failures = []\n    for r in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        try:\n            process(r)\n        except Exception:\n            failures.append({&quot;itemIdentifier&quot;: r[&quot;messageId&quot;]})\n    return {&quot;batchItemFailures&quot;: failures}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0252","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"How do you implement idempotent processing for duplicate events?","a":"<p>Derive an <strong>idempotency key</strong> (message id or a hash of the payload) and record it in a store (DynamoDB with a conditional write such as <code>attribute_not_exists</code>, with TTL for cleanup). On each event try to claim the key. If it already exists, skip (return the prior result). The <strong>AWS Lambda Powertools Idempotency</strong> utility does this for you.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)\ndef already_done(key):\n    try:\n        ddb.put_item(TableName=&quot;Idem&quot;, Item={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: key}},\n                     ConditionExpression=&quot;attribute_not_exists(id)&quot;)\n        return False          # first time: safe to process\n    except ddb.exceptions.ConditionalCheckFailedException:\n        return True           # duplicate: skip</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0253","card":"q-lambda-067-a-slow-third-party-api","sec":"lambda-s067","q":"A third-party API called by Lambda takes 30 seconds and causes timeouts. How do you make it asynchronous?","a":"<p>Decouple the slow call from the caller. Accept the request, enqueue it (SQS, EventBridge or Step Functions) and return <code>202 Accepted</code> with a job id. A worker Lambda calls the third-party API. Use <strong>Step Functions</strong> with a <code>WaitForTaskToken</code> callback or polling pattern for long waits. Store the result (DynamoDB or S3) and notify through a webhook or SNS, or let the client poll a status endpoint. This removes the synchronous 30 s wait and its timeout risk.</p>\n<p>Flow: client calls API Gateway, a Lambda enqueues the job and returns 202 with a job id, SQS feeds a worker Lambda that calls the slow API and writes the result to DynamoDB, and the client polls <code>GET /status/{jobId}</code> (or receives an SNS or webhook callback).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0254","card":"q-lambda-068-function-not-invoked-by-its-event-source","sec":"lambda-s068","q":"What do you check when a Lambda function is not invoked by its event source?","a":"<p>Check the event source mapping, permissions and CloudWatch Logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0255","card":"q-lambda-068-function-not-invoked-by-its-event-source","sec":"lambda-s068","q":"Which AWS CLI commands help diagnose a Lambda function that is not invoked?","a":"<p>Get the event source mapping, the function policy and the log streams:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda get-event-source-mapping --uuid &lt;event-source-mapping-uuid&gt;\naws lambda get-policy --function-name &lt;function-name&gt;\naws logs describe-log-streams --log-group-name /aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0256","card":"q-lambda-068-function-not-invoked-by-its-event-source","sec":"lambda-s068","q":"What are the console steps to troubleshoot a Lambda function that is not invoked?","a":"<p>Lambda &gt; Functions &gt; select the function &gt; Triggers (verify the source, for example SQS) &gt; Permissions (check the execution role) &gt; Monitor &gt; Logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0257","card":"q-lambda-069-function-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s069","q":"What do you do when a Lambda function is timing out?","a":"<p>Increase the timeout, optimize the code and check logs for bottlenecks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0258","card":"q-lambda-069-function-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s069","q":"Which CLI command raises a Lambda function's timeout to 60 seconds?","a":"<p><code>update-function-configuration</code> with <code>--timeout</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --timeout 60</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0259","card":"q-lambda-069-function-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s069","q":"What are the console steps to raise a Lambda timeout?","a":"<p>Configuration &gt; General configuration &gt; Edit &gt; raise the timeout (for example 60 s), then Monitor &gt; Logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0260","card":"q-lambda-070-function-not-processing-sqs-events","sec":"lambda-s070","q":"What do you verify when a Lambda function is not processing SQS queue events?","a":"<p>Verify the SQS trigger, permissions (<code>sqs:ReceiveMessage</code>) and the queue visibility timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0261","card":"q-lambda-070-function-not-processing-sqs-events","sec":"lambda-s070","q":"Which CLI commands diagnose a Lambda function that does not process SQS events?","a":"<p>List the event source mappings, get the function policy and read the queue's <code>VisibilityTimeout</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda list-event-source-mappings --function-name &lt;function-name&gt;\naws lambda get-policy --function-name &lt;function-name&gt;\naws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url &lt;queue-url&gt; --attribute-names VisibilityTimeout</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0262","card":"q-lambda-071-multiple-invocations-for-one-event","sec":"lambda-s071","q":"What do you do when a Lambda function runs several times for a single event?","a":"<p>Look for more than one event source for the function, add idempotency and adjust retries. In SQS adjust the delivery delay or visibility timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0263","card":"q-lambda-071-multiple-invocations-for-one-event","sec":"lambda-s071","q":"Which CLI command helps find duplicate event source mappings for a function?","a":"<p>List its event source mappings:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda list-event-source-mappings --function-name &lt;function-name&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0264","card":"q-lambda-071-multiple-invocations-for-one-event","sec":"lambda-s071","q":"How can a Lambda handler start to guard against duplicate events?","a":"<p>Read the event ID and check it in DynamoDB:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>event_id = event.get(&#x27;id&#x27;)\n# Check event_id in DynamoDB</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0265","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"What do you do about a Lambda runtime error?","a":"<p>Enable logging, review logs and fix the code. Add try/except with logging and reproduce with the Test tab.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0266","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"Which CLI command lists the log streams of a Lambda function?","a":"<p><code>describe-log-streams</code> on the function's log group:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs describe-log-streams --log-group-name /aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0267","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"How does a Python Lambda handler log errors and re-raise them?","a":"<p>It sets up a logger, catches the exception, logs the error and raises it again:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nlogger = logging.getLogger()\nlogger.setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        logger.info(&quot;Processing event&quot;)\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Error: {str(e)}&quot;)\n        raise e</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0268","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"How do you fix a \"Permission denied\" error for S3 in Lambda?","a":"<p>Add S3 permissions to the execution role (Lambda &gt; Permissions &gt; Execution role &gt; IAM console &gt; inline policy).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0269","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"Which CLI command grants a Lambda role <code>s3:GetObject</code> and <code>s3:PutObject</code> on a bucket?","a":"<p><code>put-role-policy</code> with an inline policy:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam put-role-policy --role-name &lt;lambda-role-name&gt; --policy-name s3-access --policy-document &#x27;{\n    &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n    &quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;, &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;], &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::&lt;bucket-name&gt;/*&quot;}]\n}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0270","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"What do you fix when Lambda cannot assume its execution role?","a":"<p>Fix the trust policy so it names the Lambda service (IAM &gt; Roles &gt; role &gt; Trust relationships &gt; Edit).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0271","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"Which CLI command fixes the trust policy of a Lambda execution role?","a":"<p><code>update-assume-role-policy</code>, allowing <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code> to <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam update-assume-role-policy --role-name &lt;lambda-role-name&gt; --policy-document &#x27;{\n    &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n    &quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: {&quot;Service&quot;: &quot;lambda.amazonaws.com&quot;}, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;}]\n}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0272","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"What do you add when Lambda cannot publish to SNS?","a":"<p>Add <code>sns:Publish</code> on the topic to the role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0273","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"Which CLI command lets a Lambda role publish to an SNS topic?","a":"<p><code>put-role-policy</code> allowing <code>sns:Publish</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam put-role-policy --role-name &lt;lambda-role-name&gt; --policy-name sns-access --policy-document &#x27;{\n    &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n    &quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;sns:Publish&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:sns:&lt;region&gt;:&lt;account-id&gt;:&lt;topic-name&gt;&quot;}]\n}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0274","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"What do you do about high Lambda latency?","a":"<p>Enable X-Ray, optimize code and increase memory. Console: Configuration &gt; Monitoring and operations tools &gt; Enable X-Ray, then General configuration &gt; memory (for example 512 MB), then analyze traces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0275","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"Which CLI commands enable active X-Ray tracing and set memory to 512 MB?","a":"<p>Two <code>update-function-configuration</code> calls:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --tracing-config Mode=Active\naws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --memory-size 512</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0276","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"What do you do when a Lambda function hits the concurrency limit?","a":"<p>Check metrics, set reserved concurrency and optimize execution time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0277","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"Which CLI command sets reserved concurrency of 100 for a function?","a":"<p><code>put-function-concurrency</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --reserved-concurrent-executions 100</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0278","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"What do you do about high Lambda cold start times?","a":"<p>Enable Provisioned Concurrency, use a lightweight runtime and optimize initialization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0279","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"Which CLI command sets Provisioned Concurrency of 5 on an alias or version?","a":"<p><code>put-provisioned-concurrency-config</code> with <code>--qualifier</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-provisioned-concurrency-config --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --qualifier &lt;alias-or-version&gt; --provisioned-concurrent-executions 5</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0280","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"What do you check when Lambda errors are not logged?","a":"<p>Confirm the role allows <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>, and add logging in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0281","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"How does a Python Lambda handler log a result and an error with stack trace?","a":"<p>With <code>logging.info</code> on success and <code>logging.error(..., exc_info=True)</code> before re-raising:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nlogging.getLogger().setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        result = some_operation()\n        logging.info(f&quot;Result: {result}&quot;)\n        return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 200, &quot;body&quot;: json.dumps(result)}\n    except Exception as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Error: {str(e)}&quot;, exc_info=True)\n        raise</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0282","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"What do you do when a Lambda function keeps retrying indefinitely?","a":"<p>Set a retry limit and configure a DLQ (Configuration &gt; Asynchronous invocation).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0283","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"Which CLI command sets 2 maximum retries and a dead-letter queue on a function?","a":"<p><code>update-function-configuration</code> with <code>--maximum-retry-attempts</code> and <code>--dead-letter-config</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name my-function --maximum-retry-attempts 2 --dead-letter-config TargetArn=arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:123456789012:my-dlq-queue</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0284","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"What do you do when unexpected messages appear in a dead-letter queue?","a":"<p>Check logs, verify retries and inspect the event source and the DLQ messages. Log the event so you can see what failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0285","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"How do you log the incoming event in a Python handler for DLQ debugging?","a":"<p>Log <code>json.dumps(event)</code> with <code>logging.info</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>logging.info(f&quot;Event: {json.dumps(event)}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0286","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"What do you verify when Lambda is not receiving EventBridge events?","a":"<p>Verify the rule targets the function, the function policy allows <code>events.amazonaws.com</code>, and check logs for invocation errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0287","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"Which CLI commands check an EventBridge rule and a function's policy?","a":"<p>Describe the rule and get the policy:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws events describe-rule --name my-rule\naws lambda get-policy --function-name my-function</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0288","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"Which statement lets EventBridge invoke a Lambda function?","a":"<p>Allow <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code> with Principal <code>events.amazonaws.com</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:my-function&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: &quot;events.amazonaws.com&quot;}]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0289","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"What do you verify when Lambda fails to write to Kinesis?","a":"<p>Verify the role has <code>kinesis:PutRecord</code> and <code>kinesis:PutRecords</code>, the stream name and the code. Test with a sample event.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0290","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"How does a Python Lambda handler write a record to a Kinesis stream?","a":"<p>With <code>kinesis.put_record</code>, giving <code>StreamName</code>, <code>Data</code> and <code>PartitionKey</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = kinesis.put_record(\n    StreamName=&quot;my-stream&quot;,\n    Data=json.dumps({&quot;data&quot;: &quot;example&quot;}),\n    PartitionKey=&quot;partition-key&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0291","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"Which IAM statement lets a Lambda role write to a Kinesis stream?","a":"<p>Allow <code>kinesis:PutRecord</code> and <code>kinesis:PutRecords</code> on the stream ARN:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;kinesis:PutRecord&quot;, &quot;kinesis:PutRecords&quot;], &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:kinesis:us-east-1:123456789012:stream/my-stream&quot;}]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0292","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"What do you verify when Lambda is not processing S3 events?","a":"<p>Verify the bucket event notification (S3 &gt; bucket &gt; Properties &gt; Event notifications &gt; add the Lambda trigger), the function policy allowing <code>s3.amazonaws.com</code>, and logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0293","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"Which CLI command adds an S3 notification that invokes a Lambda function on object creation?","a":"<p><code>put-bucket-notification-configuration</code> with <code>LambdaFunctionConfigurations</code> and the event <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws s3api put-bucket-notification-configuration --bucket my-bucket --notification-configuration &#x27;{\n    &quot;LambdaFunctionConfigurations&quot;: [{&quot;LambdaFunctionArn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:my-function&quot;, &quot;Events&quot;: [&quot;s3:ObjectCreated:*&quot;]}]\n}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0294","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"Which statement lets S3 invoke a Lambda function?","a":"<p>Allow <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code> with Principal <code>s3.amazonaws.com</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:my-function&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: &quot;s3.amazonaws.com&quot;}]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0295","card":"q-lambda-077-failing-to-access-rds","sec":"lambda-s077","q":"What do you verify when Lambda fails to access RDS?","a":"<p>Verify the VPC config (correct VPC, subnets and security groups), that the RDS security group allows the DB port (for example 3306), and IAM permissions. The role also needs the ENI actions for VPC access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0296","card":"q-lambda-077-failing-to-access-rds","sec":"lambda-s077","q":"How does a Python Lambda handler connect to RDS MySQL?","a":"<p>With <code>pymysql.connect</code>, taking host, user and password from environment variables:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conn = pymysql.connect(\n    host=os.environ[&quot;RDS_HOST&quot;],\n    user=os.environ[&quot;RDS_USER&quot;],\n    password=os.environ[&quot;RDS_PASSWORD&quot;],\n    database=&quot;my_database&quot;\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0297","card":"q-lambda-077-failing-to-access-rds","sec":"lambda-s077","q":"Which IAM actions does a Lambda role need to connect to RDS inside a VPC?","a":"<p><code>rds-db:connect</code> on the database, and the ENI actions <code>ec2:CreateNetworkInterface</code>, <code>ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces</code> and <code>ec2:DeleteNetworkInterface</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;rds-db:connect&quot;], &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:rds:us-east-1:123456789012:db:my-database&quot;},\n{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;ec2:CreateNetworkInterface&quot;, &quot;ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces&quot;, &quot;ec2:DeleteNetworkInterface&quot;], &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0298","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What are the Lambda timeout and memory limits?","a":"<p>Max timeout 900 s (15 min). Memory 128 MB to 10,240 MB, which also scales CPU and network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0299","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What are the Lambda <code>/tmp</code> storage limits?","a":"<p>512 MB default, 10,240 MB (10 GB) max.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0300","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What are the Lambda payload limits for sync and async invocation?","a":"<p>Sync: 6 MB (request and response). Async: 256 KB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0301","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What are the Lambda package size limits?","a":"<p>Zip: 50 MB direct upload, 250 MB unzipped (code and layers). Container image: up to 10 GB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0302","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"How many layers can a Lambda function have?","a":"<ol start=\"5\">\n  <li>They count toward the 250 MB unzip limit.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0303","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What is the default regional Lambda concurrency, and is it soft or hard?","a":"<p>1,000, a soft limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0304","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"How many times does Lambda retry an async invocation automatically?","a":"<p>2 retries (3 attempts in total), then the event goes to the DLQ or a destination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0305","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What is the size limit for Lambda environment variables?","a":"<p>512 KB total.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0306","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What is the Step Functions state payload limit?","a":"<p>256 KB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0307","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"What is the Lambda billing granularity?","a":"<p>Per 1 ms x memory (GB-seconds) plus per request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0308","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What are the key characteristics of Lambda in one line?","a":"<p>Serverless, event-driven, stateless, per-ms billing, Firecracker microVMs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0309","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What belongs in Init, Invoke and Shutdown in Lambda?","a":"<p>Init once (cache clients), Invoke per event, Shutdown cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0310","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What are the cold start fixes?","a":"<p>Provisioned Concurrency, SnapStart, smaller package, lazy load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0311","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"Which Lambda setting is the CPU dial, and what tunes it?","a":"<p>Memory is the CPU dial. Tune it with Power Tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0312","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"How do sync, async and ESM invocations differ in Lambda?","a":"<p>Sync (API Gateway, ALB) waits. Async (S3, SNS, EventBridge) retries twice, then DLQ or destination. ESM (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB) polls batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0313","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What is the difference between a DLQ and destinations in Lambda?","a":"<p>A DLQ holds the failed payload. Destinations route success and failure with full context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0314","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What do the execution role and resource policy control?","a":"<p>The execution role is outbound. The resource policy is inbound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0315","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"Where should Lambda secrets go?","a":"<p>In Secrets Manager or SSM, fetched at Init.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0316","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What does a Lambda function in a VPC need for networking?","a":"<p>A VPC needs ENIs (Hyperplane), NAT for internet and endpoints for private AWS access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0317","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"How do you compute Lambda concurrency, and how do reserved and provisioned concurrency differ?","a":"<p>Concurrency = requests per second x duration. Reserved caps and guarantees. Provisioned pre-warms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0318","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"What are the Lambda observability tools?","a":"<p>CloudWatch metrics and logs, X-Ray, Logs Insights, structured JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0319","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"How do you deploy Lambda changes safely?","a":"<p>Deploy with layers, versions and aliases, CodeDeploy canary or linear, and SAM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0320","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"Which common Lambda patterns are worth recalling?","a":"<p>Fan-out, queue buffer, idempotency, async offload.</p>","hand":true}];
